exports[`content > writes composition as markdown 1`] = `
"# composition

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

render the zriz parts as your own components, and add your own handlers next to zriz's.

## render your own element

every part takes \`render\`. pass an element, and zriz renders that element with the part's props, ref and data attributes merged in. your component must spread the props it gets onto its root element and forward its ref. this one uses \`forwardRef\`, which works in react 18 and 19. in react 19 you can take \`ref\` as a plain prop instead:

\`\`\`tsx
import { forwardRef } from "react";
import type { ComponentPropsWithoutRef } from "react";

type IconButtonProps = ComponentPropsWithoutRef<"button"> & {
  icon: string;
};

export const IconButton = forwardRef<
  HTMLButtonElement,
  IconButtonProps
>(function IconButton({ icon, className, ...props }, ref) {
  return (
    <button
      ref={ref}
      className={\`icon-button \${className ?? ""}\`}
      data-icon={icon}
      {...props}
    />
  );
});
\`\`\`

then pass it to a part:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Rotate
  aria-label="Rotate left"
  render={<IconButton icon="rotate-left" />}
/>
\`\`\`

the element's own props win over zriz's, except \`className\` and \`style\`, which merge, and event handlers, which both run. a prop set to \`undefined\` keeps zriz's value.

## change the element by state

pass a function instead, and it gets the part's props and its state. spread the props onto what you return, because they carry the ref and the handlers:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Flip
  aria-label="Flip"
  render={(props, state) => (
    <IconButton
      {...props}
      icon={state.mirrored ? "flip-on" : "flip"}
    />
  )}
/>
\`\`\`

each part's state is in the api table on its page. \`className\` takes the same state, as shown in [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md).

## stop zriz's own handler

your handler runs first. call \`preventZrizHandler(event)\` in it, and zriz skips that part's own handler for that event. a press still bubbles to \`Viewport\`, so veto there to stop a pan, or call \`event.stopPropagation()\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Reset
  onClick={(event) => {
    if (!window.confirm("discard your changes?")) {
      preventZrizHandler(event);
    }
  }}
>
  reset
</Cropper.Reset>
\`\`\`

\`event.preventDefault()\` does not stop zriz. it only stops the browser default.

\`preventZrizHandler\`, \`mergeProps\`, \`mergeRefs\` and \`CropperContext\` come from \`@zriz/react\`, like the hooks.

## build a part of your own

a part of your own reads the crop with the hooks, or takes the cropper itself from \`CropperContext\`, and renders with \`useRender\`, which applies \`render\` and \`className\` the way the zriz parts do. [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md) shows one. \`mergeProps\` merges your props with a caller's and chains handlers the same way, and \`mergeRefs\` joins a ref you hold with the one a caller passes. \`CropperContext\` is \`null\` outside \`Root\`.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes crop-area as markdown 1`] = `
"# crop area

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Area\` is the crop you keep. \`Handle\` resizes it and \`Grid\` draws guides while you work.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.

## anatomy

\`Area\` goes in \`Viewport\`. \`Grid\` and the handles go inside it:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
  <Cropper.Grid />
  <Cropper.Handle position="top-left" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="top" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="top-right" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="right" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-right" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="bottom" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-left" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="left" />
</Cropper.Area>
\`\`\`

leave out the handles you do not need. with \`shape="circle"\`, the stylesheet hides the four corner handles.

\`Area\` points \`aria-describedby\` at the \`Instructions\` part. render it with your own help text. without it, the reference points at nothing and screen readers skip it. [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) covers it and \`Announcer\`.

## add a focus ring

the browser draws its default ring on \`Area\`. to draw your own:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="area"]:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #fdca00;
  outline-offset: 4px;
}
\`\`\`

## make the handles easier to grab

each handle's hit box is 44 px square, centred on the crop's edge. set \`--zriz-handle-size\` on \`Area\` to change all eight. give the viewport padding of at least half that size, or the clip cuts the box, see [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="viewport"] {
  padding: 28px;
}
[data-zriz-item="area"] {
  --zriz-handle-size: 56px;
}
\`\`\`

## know when the grid shows

the grid hides at rest. it shows thirds (3 × 3) while you move the crop and a fine grid (9 × 9) while you drag the dial:

| when | what shows |
| - | - |
| move the crop | the thirds grid fades in. a drag, pinch, twist, wheel, handle, \`Zoom\` or a \`useZoom\` slider counts |
| drag the dial | the fine grid fades in |
| press a key on \`Area\`, \`Dial\` or \`Zoom\` | no new grid. a grid that already shows stays |
| stop moving the crop | it stays for a short hold, then fades out, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) |
| let go of the dial | the fine grid fades out at once, or when a flick's glide stops |

## make the straighten grid fainter

lines of the fine grid carry no \`data-coarse\`. a fading grid keeps its lines, so this rule holds until the grid is gone:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="grid"] > :not([data-coarse]) {
  background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.3);
}
\`\`\`

## keyboard

these keys work while \`Area\` has focus, never on the window:

| key | what it does |
| - | - |
| \`arrow keys\` | move the crop by 1% of its size |
| \`shift\` + \`arrow keys\` | move it by 10% |
| \`alt\` + \`arrow keys\` | resize the crop by 1%, or 10% with shift. right and down grow it, left and up shrink it. a locked aspect keeps its ratio. a free crop changes that side only, and stops at the photo edge, \`minSize\` or 88 px on screen. the centre stays put |
| \`+\` or \`=\` | zoom in 1.1× |
| \`-\` or \`_\` | zoom out 1.1× |
| \`0\` | jump back to where \`Reset\` goes, with no spring |

escape during a handle drag cancels it and puts the crop back.

## api

### \`Area\`

renders a \`<div role="group">\` with \`tabIndex={0}\` inside \`Viewport\`. zriz writes its position and size inline on every frame. every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ shape, interacting }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  names the crop for screen readers, such as \`"Crop area"\`. development builds warn without it
- \`liveVariables\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  writes the variables below on \`Area\` every frame

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-shape\` | \`"rect"\` or \`"circle"\`, from \`Root\` |
| \`data-interacting\` | during a gesture, and while a zriz key is held down |

| variable | what it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-line\` | paints the frame and the handle marks. [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |
| \`--zriz-rotation\` | with \`liveVariables\`, the straighten angle in degrees, like −4.5 |
| \`--zriz-rotation-progress\` | with \`liveVariables\`, the angle over its limit, from −1 to 1 |
| \`--zriz-zoom\` | with \`liveVariables\`, the zoom, 1 to \`maxZoom\` |
| \`--zriz-zoom-progress\` | with \`liveVariables\`, the zoom on a log scale, 0 to 1 |
| \`--zriz-overscroll\` | with \`liveVariables\`, how far in px a drag pulls the photo past an edge. 0 at rest |

### \`Handle\`

renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` inside \`Area\`. the stylesheet hides the corner handles on a circle only when they sit right inside \`Area\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ position, active }\`.

- \`position\` \`HandleEdge\` required

  the corner or edge it drags: \`"top-left"\`, \`"top"\`, \`"top-right"\`, \`"right"\`, \`"bottom-right"\`, \`"bottom"\`, \`"bottom-left"\`, \`"left"\`

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-edge\` | always, set to its position |
| \`data-active\` | while this handle is dragged |

| variable | what it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-handle-size\` | the hit box, 44 px square unless you set it |

### \`Grid\`

renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` of \`<span>\` lines inside \`Area\`. it has no props of its own. the stylesheet gives each line 1 px. without it, set \`min-width\` and \`min-height\` yourself. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ grid }\`.

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-grid\` | \`"coarse"\` for thirds, \`"fine"\` or \`"none"\` |
| \`data-visible\` | while a grid shows |
| \`data-axis\` | \`"x"\` on vertical lines, \`"y"\` on horizontal ones |
| \`data-coarse\` | on each line of the thirds grid |

| variable | what it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-grid\` | paints the grid lines. [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its default |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes cropper as markdown 1`] = `
"# cropper

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`createCropper\` makes the store behind every \`Root\`. use it for [vanilla js](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md), or to test your crop logic without a browser.

import it from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md), give it the photo's size and write to it:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
});
cropper.setRotation(7);
\`\`\`

a write changes \`getValue()\` at once. \`change\` and \`commit\` fire on the next frame. in react, \`Root\` makes the cropper for you, and [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md) shows how to reach it.

## options

- \`image\` \`Size | null\` = \`null\`

  sets the photo's natural size. most writes wait for it
- \`value\` \`CropValue\`

  sets the start in place of \`defaultValue\`. \`reset()\` still goes to \`defaultValue\`
- \`defaultValue\` \`Partial<CropValue>\`

  merges over the core \`defaultValue\`. the cropper starts here and \`reset()\` returns here. read once
- \`minSize\` \`Size\` = \`{ width: 1, height: 1 }\`

  sets the smallest crop in source pixels
- \`maxZoom\` \`number\` = \`15\`

  sets the largest zoom. 1 is the largest crop
- \`focalLength\` \`number\` = \`26\`

  sets the lens as a 35 mm equivalent, for perspective correction. at a strong tilt, zriz lengthens a lens too short to keep the photo in front of it
- \`disabled\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  refuses gestures and most writes
- \`clock\` \`Clock\` = \`frameClock()\`

  schedules frames. tests pass a manual one, see test without a browser below
- \`onValueChange\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  runs at most once per frame while the crop moves
- \`onValueCommit\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  runs when a gesture or write commits
- \`plugins\` \`readonly CropperPlugin[]\` = \`[]\`

  adds behaviour, see add a plugin and clean it up below

\`minSize\` also caps the angle on tiny photos. while \`disabled\`, \`setValue()\`, \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\` still work. both callbacks are on [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md).

## read the crop

- \`getValue\` \`() => CropValue\`

  returns the live [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md), frames inside a gesture included
- \`getState\` \`() => CropperSnapshot\`

  returns the snapshot that \`useCropperState\` reads, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md)
- \`getCropState\` \`() => CropState | null\`

  returns the crop in image pixels, or \`null\` before a photo
- \`getZoom\` \`() => number\`

  returns the zoom, 1 before a photo loads. 1 is the largest crop that fits
- \`getMaxZoom\` \`() => number\`

  returns the \`maxZoom\` option, 15 by default
- \`getRotationLimit\` \`() => number\`

  returns 45, or less when \`minSize\` leaves too little room
- \`getPixelCrop\` \`(scale?) => ExportPlan | null\`

  returns the export plan, or \`null\` before a photo, see [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md)

## change the crop

- \`setValue\` \`(value, options?) => void\`

  merges the fields you pass into the current value
- \`setRotation\` \`(degrees, options?) => void\`

  straightens the photo
- \`setZoom\` \`(zoom, options?) => void\`

  zooms around \`anchor\`, a point from 0 to 1 on the photo, or the crop's centre
- \`panBy\` \`(dx, dy, options?) => void\`

  moves the crop by fractions of the photo's width and height
- \`setAspect\` \`(aspect, options?) => void\`

  locks the crop to an aspect
- \`setCropState\` \`(state, options?) => void\`

  sets the crop in image pixels. \`keep: "centre"\` holds the centre. the default, \`keep: "size"\`, holds the size
- \`rotate90\` \`(direction?, options?) => void\`

  turns counter-clockwise by default, or clockwise with \`"cw"\`. a numeric aspect turns with it, so 4:3 becomes 3:4
- \`flip\` \`(axis?, options?) => void\`

  mirrors left to right by default. \`"y"\` mirrors top to bottom
- \`reset\` \`(options?) => void\`

  returns to \`defaultValue\`, with or without a photo
- \`setImage\` \`(size | null) => void\`

  sets the photo's natural size. a new size refits the crop with reason \`coverage\`. a size that is not finite and positive counts as \`null\`
- \`setOptions\` \`(options) => void\`

  changes \`disabled\`, \`maxZoom\`, \`minSize\`, \`focalLength\` and the two callbacks. \`image\`, \`value\`, \`defaultValue\`, \`clock\` and \`plugins\` stay as created. a new \`focalLength\` takes effect on the next \`setValue()\`, \`reset()\` or \`setImage()\`. call \`setImage()\` for a new photo

every write keeps the crop valid, see [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md). before the cropper has a photo, only \`setValue()\`, \`reset()\`, \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\` do anything.

## tell listeners why

every write except \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\` takes action options last. outside a gesture, \`setValue()\`, \`setRotation()\`, \`setZoom()\` and \`setCropState()\` report \`imperative\` unless you pass a reason. here the write straightens the photo 5°, and on the next frame the commit logs \`5 history\`:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
  onValueCommit: (value, details) => {
    console.log(value.rotation, details.reason);
  },
});
cropper.setRotation(5, { reason: "history" });
\`\`\`

- \`reason\` \`ChangeReason\`

  names the change for listeners, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md)
- \`event\` \`Event\`

  passes the dom event behind the change
- \`emit\` \`boolean\`

  with \`false\`, no change or commit fires, and the new value counts as committed. it also drops a change still waiting from the same frame

## drive the crop from your own input

call \`begin()\` when your input takes hold and \`end()\` when it lets go. every write between them is one gesture with one commit. pass the same \`owner\` object to both, so no other input can end your gesture:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
});
const owner = {};

export const bind = (input: HTMLInputElement) => {
  input.addEventListener("pointerdown", (event) => {
    cropper.begin("dial", event, owner);
  });
  input.addEventListener("input", () => {
    cropper.setRotation(input.valueAsNumber);
  });
  const end = (event: PointerEvent) => {
    cropper.end(event, owner);
  };
  input.addEventListener("pointerup", end);
  input.addEventListener("pointercancel", end);
};
\`\`\`

- \`begin\` \`(reason, event?, owner?) => boolean\`

  starts a gesture and stops any animation that holds the commit. returns \`false\` when the cropper is disabled or another owner holds a gesture
- \`end\` \`(event?, owner?) => void\`

  ends the gesture. the commit follows once blocking tasks stop
- \`cancel\` \`(event?, owner?) => void\`

  ends the gesture and restores the crop from before it
- \`run\` \`(task, blocking?) => () => void\`

  calls \`task(time, delta)\` every frame until it returns \`false\`. blocking tasks, the default, hold the commit back. returns a cancel function
- \`invalidate\` \`() => void\`

  asks for one more \`frame\` event
- \`on\` \`(type, listener) => () => void\`

  listens to one event, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md). returns a remove function
- \`subscribe\` \`(listener) => () => void\`

  runs the listener when the snapshot changes

## test without a browser

in node, the default clock waits about 16 ms per frame on \`setTimeout\`. pass a clock of your own and step the frames yourself, so every event fires when your test says:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";
import type { Clock } from "@zriz/core";

const queue = new Map<number, (time: number) => void>();
let id = 0;
let now = 0;

const clock: Clock = {
  now: () => now,
  request: (step) => {
    id += 1;
    queue.set(id, step);
    return id;
  },
  cancel: (handle) => {
    queue.delete(handle);
  },
};

const advance = (ms: number) => {
  now += ms;
  const steps = [...queue.values()];
  queue.clear();
  for (const step of steps) step(now);
};

const commits: number[] = [];
const cropper = createCropper({
  clock,
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
  onValueCommit: (value) => commits.push(value.rotation),
});

cropper.setRotation(7);
advance(16);
console.log(commits);
\`\`\`

\`setRotation()\` changes \`getValue()\` at once, and \`advance(16)\` runs the frame that sends the commit. the log reads \`[7]\`.

## add a plugin and clean it up

a plugin is an object with a \`name\`, an \`init\` and an optional \`destroy\`. \`init\` runs right after \`createCropper\` makes the cropper. \`Root\` and \`useCropper\` take \`plugins\` too, read them once and run \`init\` once on the client, after mount, never during a render.

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";
import type { CropperPlugin } from "@zriz/core";

let off = () => {};

const logger: CropperPlugin = {
  name: "logger",
  init: (cropper) => {
    off = cropper.on("commit", (value) => console.log(value));
  },
  destroy: () => off(),
};

const cropper = createCropper({ plugins: [logger] });
cropper.destroy();
\`\`\`

\`destroy\` runs only when you call \`destroy()\`. \`Root\` never calls it. when the component that made the cropper unmounts, \`Root\` or the one that called \`useCropper\`, it calls \`stop()\` instead, on purpose: \`StrictMode\` and \`<Activity>\` unmount a component and mount the same cropper again, and a destroyed cropper cannot come back.

if a plugin holds a timer or a socket, call \`destroy()\` yourself when you are done with the cropper, for example through \`actionsRef\`.

- \`stop\` \`() => void\`

  ends any gesture, drops all motion, commits what is there and emits \`stop\`. the cropper keeps working
- \`destroy\` \`() => void\`

  runs each plugin's \`destroy\`, emits \`destroy\`, detaches the photo and drops every listener. the cropper is finished, and every later write is ignored
"
`;

exports[`content > writes dial as markdown 1`] = `
"# dial

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

the ruler that levels a tilted photo, up to 45° each way, like the one in ios photos.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/dial.

## anatomy

put \`DialTicks\` inside \`Dial\`. \`Dial\` and \`DialValue\` go anywhere inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.DialValue />
<Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
  <Cropper.DialTicks />
</Cropper.Dial>
\`\`\`

the stylesheet makes \`Dial\` 40 px tall and clips its ticks at the sides only, so the zero dot above them stays in view. it draws the ticks and the centre line. \`DialValue\` ships bare.

## drag and flick the ruler

drag the ruler 4 px to turn 1°. a quick flick keeps it gliding after you let go, and past the limit it stretches and springs back. on touch, a vertical swipe scrolls the page instead.

## set the ruler width

\`Dial\` fills its parent's width. ticks sit 9 px apart, one every 2.25°, so 361 px fits all 41 ticks:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten" style={{ width: 361 }}>
  <Cropper.DialTicks />
</Cropper.Dial>
\`\`\`

## add a degree sign

\`format\` on \`DialValue\` gets the angle in degrees and returns the text to show:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.DialValue
  format={(angle) => \`\${Math.round(angle)}°\`}
/>
\`\`\`

## make the major ticks taller

ticks carry no \`data-zriz-item\`. select them through \`DialTicks\`:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="dial-ticks"] > [data-major] {
  height: 14px;
}
\`\`\`

## change the drag colour

the centre line takes the accent colour while you drag. \`--zriz-accent\` sets it, and [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its default:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="dial"] {
  --zriz-accent: #0a84ff;
}
\`\`\`

## keyboard

these keys work while \`Dial\` has focus:

| key | what it does |
| - | - |
| \`left\` or \`right\` | lower or raise the angle by 1° |
| \`up\` or \`down\` | raise or lower the angle by 1° |
| \`shift\` + \`arrow keys\` | the same by 10° |
| \`page up\` or \`page down\` | raise or lower the angle by 10° |
| \`home\` or \`end\` | go to the lowest or the highest angle, −45° or 45° at most |
| \`0\` | straighten to 0° |

with \`dir="rtl"\` on \`Root\`, left and right swap. up and down stay the same.

## api

### \`Dial\`

renders a \`<div role="slider">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ dragging }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  names the slider for screen readers. development builds warn without it
- \`format\` \`Intl.NumberFormatOptions\`

  how screen readers hear the angle, merged over the default \`"5 degrees"\`

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-dragging\` | while a pointer drags the ruler |
| \`data-snapped\` | while the angle rests at exactly 0°, from the start too. the zero dot hides then. a drag through 0° leaves it as it was until you let go |

| variable | what it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-line\` | paints the centre line, the zero dot and the major ticks. [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |
| \`--zriz-accent\` | paints the centre line while you drag. [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its default |

### \`DialTicks\`

renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` of \`<span>\` ticks inside \`Dial\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ count }\`.

- \`count\` \`number\` = \`41\`

  how many ticks to draw, 2.25° apart. 41 spans 45° each way. use an odd count so one tick sits at 0°

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-major\` | every tenth tick from the centre: 0°, 22.5° and 45° each way |
| \`data-zero\` | the centre tick, at 0° |
| \`data-fade\` | \`"0"\`, \`"1"\` or \`"2"\` on the last three ticks at each end, outermost \`"0"\` |

| variable | what it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-tick\` | paints the minor ticks. [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |
| \`--zriz-tick-index\` | on each tick, its index from the left, from 0 to \`count\` minus 1 |

### \`DialValue\`

renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<span>\` anywhere inside \`Root\`.

- \`format\` \`(angle: number) => string\`

  turns the angle into the text shown. the default rounds it and adds no unit
"
`;

exports[`content > writes errors as markdown 1`] = `
"# errors

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

every error zriz throws or reports, when it happens and how to fix it.

## where errors arrive

\`Root\` calls \`onError\` with a \`ZrizError\` when a photo cannot load. \`ZrizError\` comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`, like \`loadImage\` and \`exportCrop\`. \`loadImage\` and \`exportCrop\` reject instead, so catch them where you call them:

\`\`\`ts
import type { Cropper } from "@zriz/core";
import { exportCrop, ZrizError } from "@zriz/core/dom";

export const save = async (
  cropper: Cropper,
  signal?: AbortSignal
) => {
  try {
    const { blob } = await exportCrop(cropper, { signal });
    return blob;
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      error instanceof ZrizError &&
      error.code === "aborted"
    ) {
      return null;
    }
    throw error;
  }
};
\`\`\`

every \`ZrizError\` has a \`code\` from the table below, and its message starts with \`zriz:\`. the one exception is a \`decode\` function you pass to \`loadImage\`: when it throws, \`loadImage\` rejects with your own error as it is.

## codes

| code | when it fires, and the fix |
| - | - |
| \`cross-origin\` | the server sent the photo without \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`, the network dropped, \`connect-src\` in a content security policy blocked the url, or an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` you pass is tainted. send the header. if the request needs cookies, pass \`crossOrigin="use-credentials"\` to \`Root\`. add the host to \`connect-src\`, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) |
| \`unsupported-format\` | the browser could not decode a heic, jpeg xl or tiff file, because it does not support the format or the file is damaged, either on load or when an export decodes the original again. try another copy of the file. call \`loadImage\` yourself with your own \`decode\` function, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md), or convert the file to jpeg first |
| \`decode\` | the url answered with an error status, the file cannot be read, the bytes are not an image, safari refused a bmp with a v4 or v5 header, a zriz chunk failed to download, an \`img\`, \`video\` or \`canvas\` you pass cannot be read, a \`video\` has no current frame, \`img-src\` in a content security policy blocked an svg or a display-p3 export, the browser gave no 2d canvas, or an export could not decode the original again. open the url and check the response. if you are offline, try again once the network is back. if the same error comes back, reload the page, because a browser can keep a failed chunk until then. wait for \`loadeddata\` before you pass a \`video\`. [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) lists the \`img-src\` sources |
| \`timeout\` | a photo took over 15 s to load, or an export took over 30 s. time spent waiting behind other photos or exports does not count. send a smaller file or export a smaller \`maxSize\`. for a slow network, load the photo yourself with a longer \`timeout\`, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) |
| \`aborted\` | the \`signal\` you passed fired, or a new photo replaced the one being exported. \`Root\` also aborts its own load when \`src\` changes and never reports it. nothing. skip this code in your catch |
| \`blank\` | the browser drew an empty canvas at the export size, then at half and a quarter of it. this is the canvas memory limit, mostly on phones. set \`maxSize\` to 2048 by 2048 or less |
| \`no-image\` | an export ran before a photo loaded, the app attached a photo that was already freed, or the app has two copies of \`@zriz/core\` and the export came from the one that never got the photo. disable the export button until \`onLoad\` fires. load a freed photo again. keep \`@zriz/core\` at the same version as \`@zriz/react\` |

## thrown errors

these are plain \`Error\` objects that react shows in its error overlay. they mean a part or hook cannot find its cropper.

- \`zriz: parts must be inside <Cropper.Root>\`

  move the part inside \`Root\`. a part in a portal still needs \`Root\` above it in the react tree
- \`zriz: pass a cropper or render inside <Cropper.Root>\`

  a hook such as \`useCropperValue\` or \`useZoom\` ran outside \`Root\`. pass it the cropper from \`useCropper\`, or move the component inside \`Root\`
- \`zriz: no cropper\`

  \`Root\` got \`cropper\` on its first render and none later. keep passing the same cropper, or give \`Root\` a new \`key\` to start a new one

## console warnings

in development, \`Area\`, \`Dial\` and \`Zoom\` each warn once when nothing names them, with the part's name in the message. production builds print nothing.

- \`zriz: <Cropper.Area> needs aria-label, aria-labelledby or text. zriz ships no words, so screen readers get nothing otherwise.\`

  name the part. [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) shows how to label each part
"
`;

exports[`content > writes events as markdown 1`] = `
"# events

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

two callbacks on \`Root\` report crop changes: \`onValueChange\` for live ui and \`onValueCommit\` for saving.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueChange={(value) => setAngle(value.rotation)}
  onValueCommit={(value) => save(value)}
/>
\`\`\`

\`onValueChange\` runs at most once per frame while the crop moves. \`onValueCommit\` runs once when a gesture or a write is done. \`createCropper\` takes the same two options.

## save when the user lets go

a drag, a pinch or a turn of the dial commits once, when the finger lifts. a thrown dial commits when it stops. a key on \`Area\`, \`Dial\` or \`Zoom\` commits once when it comes up, however long it was held, or when focus leaves. a write such as \`rotate90()\` commits on the next frame, and the turn you see animates after it. a gesture that ends where it started does not commit. loading a photo fits the crop without a commit, so a photo the user never touches sends none. to save it anyway, read \`getValue()\` or \`getPixelCrop()\` from the cropper in \`actionsRef\`, see [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md).

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueCommit={(value, details) => {
    upload(value, details.pixelCrop);
  }}
/>
\`\`\`

## follow one gesture from start to commit

log both callbacks, then throw the dial:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueChange={(_, d) => console.log(d.reason, d.phase)}
  onValueCommit={(_, d) => console.log(d.reason, "commit")}
/>
\`\`\`

| log | when |
| - | - |
| \`dial start\` | the first frame the dial turns |
| \`dial move\` | every later frame of the drag and of the throw |
| \`dial end\` | the last frame of the throw |
| \`dial commit\` | \`onValueCommit\`, in the same frame |

a drag that stops before the finger lifts has no \`end\`, and its last \`move\` goes straight to the commit. a write outside a gesture logs one \`instant\` change, then its commit.

## reject a change

call \`details.cancel()\` and the crop snaps back to where the last change left it. this keeps the angle within 10° each way:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueChange={(value, details) => {
    if (Math.abs(value.rotation) > 10) details.cancel();
  }}
/>
\`\`\`

## change details

the second argument of \`onValueChange\`:

- \`reason\` \`ChangeReason\`

  names what moved the crop, see reasons below
- \`phase\` \`ChangePhase\`

  is \`start\`, \`move\`, \`end\` or \`instant\`, see the order above
- \`changed\` \`readonly ValueField[]\`

  lists the fields that moved. never empty
- \`previous\` \`CropValue\`

  holds what the last change left
- \`event\` \`Event | undefined\`

  holds the dom event behind the change
- \`cancel\` \`() => void\`

  rejects this change
- \`isCanceled\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` once \`cancel()\` ran

## commit details

the second argument of \`onValueCommit\`:

- \`reason\` \`ChangeReason\`

  names the gesture or write behind the commit
- \`previous\` \`CropValue\`

  holds the last committed value. push it to a stack for [undo](https://zriz.dev/docs/undo.md)
- \`pixelCrop\` \`ExportPlan | null\`

  holds the export plan at scale 1 in source pixels, or \`null\` before a photo loads. [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) cuts the original with it
- \`event\` \`Event | undefined\`

  holds the event that ended the gesture, or the one passed with the write

## reasons

every change and commit names its reason. inside a gesture, a write without its own reason reports the gesture's reason.

| reason | sent by |
| - | - |
| \`pan\` | one finger or pointer dragging the photo, and \`panBy()\` |
| \`pinch\` | two fingers pinching or twisting, or a trackpad pinch in safari |
| \`wheel\` | a mouse wheel or trackpad scrolling or zooming the photo |
| \`resize\` | a handle being dragged |
| \`dial\` | the dial being dragged or thrown |
| \`keyboard\` | keys on \`Area\` and \`Dial\`. keys on \`Zoom\` report \`zoom-control\` on each change and \`keyboard\` on the commit |
| \`zoom-control\` | the \`Zoom\` slider, \`ZoomStep\` buttons and \`useZoom\` |
| \`aspect\` | \`AspectItem\` buttons, \`setAspect()\` and the \`aspect\` prop |
| \`orientation\` | the \`Orientation\` button, which swaps portrait and landscape |
| \`rotate-90\` | \`Rotate\` buttons and \`rotate90()\` |
| \`flip\` | \`Flip\` buttons and \`flip()\` |
| \`reset\` | \`Reset\` and \`reset()\` |
| \`coverage\` | a later photo of another size, or new options, that refit the crop. the first photo sends nothing |
| \`history\` | your own writes only. zriz never sends it, so [undo](https://zriz.dev/docs/undo.md) can skip those writes. \`Root\` animates them like \`reset()\` |
| \`controlled\` | the \`rotation\` and \`zoom\` props. a new \`value\` prop sends nothing |
| \`imperative\` | \`setValue()\`, \`setRotation()\`, \`setZoom()\` and \`setCropState()\` called outside a gesture without a reason |

## listen on the cropper

the cropper emits the two callbacks as \`change\` and \`commit\`, plus six more events. in react, make the cropper with \`useCropper\` and listen in an effect: \`useEffect(() => cropper.on("settle", onSettle), [cropper])\`, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md). \`createCropper\` comes from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). \`on()\` returns a function that removes the listener:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper();
const off = cropper.on("settle", () => {
  console.log("the crop is still");
});
off();
\`\`\`

| event | listener gets | what it does |
| - | - | - |
| \`gesturestart\` | \`reason, event\` | fires when an input takes hold |
| \`gestureend\` | \`reason, event\` | fires when \`end()\` runs. for a thrown dial, that is when the throw stops |
| \`settle\` | \`nothing\` | fires each time the last animation stops, springs included |
| \`frame\` | \`{ time, value, state }\` | fires on every frame that moved. \`useCropperFrame\` wraps it |
| \`stop\` | \`nothing\` | fires when \`stop()\` ends all motion |
| \`destroy\` | \`nothing\` | fires once, when \`destroy()\` runs |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes exporting as markdown 1`] = `
"# exporting

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

turn the crop into an image file in the browser, or send the export plan to your server and cut the original there.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.

## save the crop as a file

\`exportCrop\` comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`. \`@zriz/react\` depends on \`@zriz/core\` and re-exports \`CropValue\`, \`Cropper\`, \`CropperSnapshot\`, \`Aspect\`, \`Size\`, \`ChangeDetails\` and \`CommitDetails\`. functions and the other core types, such as \`ChangeReason\` and \`ExportPlan\`, come from \`@zriz/core\`, so add it to your own dependencies:

\`\`\`sh
pnpm add @zriz/core
\`\`\`

keep it at the same version as \`@zriz/react\`. a second copy of \`@zriz/core\` cannot see the photo, see \`no-image\` on [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md).

pass a ref to \`actionsRef\` on \`Root\`, and pass \`cropper.current\` to \`exportCrop\` from a save button. the button stays disabled until \`onLoad\` fires for this file, because an export before the photo loads rejects with \`no-image\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

export const SaveCrop = ({ file }: { file: File }) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const [loaded, setLoaded] = useState<File | null>(null);

  const save = async () => {
    if (!cropper.current) return;
    const result = await exportCrop(cropper.current, {
      type: "image/webp",
      maxSize: { width: 2048, height: 2048 },
    }).catch((error: unknown) => {
      console.error(error);
      return null;
    });
    if (!result) return;
    const { blob, type } = result;
    const link = document.createElement("a");
    link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    const extension = type === "image/webp" ? "webp" : "png";
    link.download = \`crop.\${extension}\`;
    link.click();
    setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(link.href), 1000);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root
        src={file}
        actionsRef={cropper}
        onLoad={() => setLoaded(file)}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={loaded !== file}
        onClick={save}
      >
        save
      </button>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

an export can fail on a very large photo, so the snippet catches it. [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists the codes.

the export draws from the original, not the preview on screen. without \`maxSize\`, the file has the crop's size in source pixels.

## export a round avatar

with \`shape="circle"\` on \`Root\`, \`exportCrop\` reads the shape and returns a png with transparent corners. \`exportImage(cropper, image)\` exports a \`LoadedImage\` from \`loadImage\` instead. it does not read the shape, so pass \`shape: "circle"\` yourself. [avatar](https://zriz.dev/docs/avatar.md) has the whole component.

## choose size and format

- \`type\` \`"image/jpeg" | "image/png" | "image/webp"\` = \`"image/jpeg"\`

  the file format. a circle crop defaults to png
- \`quality\` \`number\` = \`0.9\`

  jpeg and webp quality, from 0 to 1
- \`maxSize\` \`{ width, height }\`

  scales the file down to fit inside this box, never up. a box with a side that is not a positive number is ignored
- \`colorSpace\` \`"srgb" | "display-p3"\` = \`"srgb"\`

  \`"display-p3"\` keeps wide colour when the photo came from a file or url. it decodes on the main thread
- \`shape\` \`"rect" | "circle"\`

  \`"rect"\` cuts a rectangle. \`"circle"\` cuts a circle with transparent corners. defaults to \`shape\` on \`Root\`
- \`signal\` \`AbortSignal\`

  cancels a running export, which then rejects with \`aborted\`

\`exportCrop\` resolves to \`{ blob, type, width, height, scaled, crop }\`. read \`type\` before you name the file, because a browser that cannot encode the type you asked for returns png. \`scaled\` is \`true\` when the file came out smaller than the crop. three things cause it: \`maxSize\`, the export size limit in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md), and the retry at half and a quarter of the size when the browser cannot draw or encode the file. \`crop\` is the export plan the file was drawn from, at the scale it came out.

exports run one at a time, in the order you call them. an aborted export stops waiting at once, but one the browser already started holds the next one back until it ends.

the browser drops exif, gps data and 16-bit depth from the file.

## export an exact size

\`maxSize\` never scales up, so a crop smaller than the box comes out smaller. set \`minSize\` on \`Root\` to the same size, and every crop is at least that big in source pixels. on a photo at least that big, a square crop with \`minSize\` and \`maxSize\` both at 512 by 512 exports at 512 by 512.

## handle a failed export

\`exportCrop\` rejects with a \`ZrizError\`. [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists each code an export can fail with, when it fires and how to fix it.

after the first photo appears, zriz loads the export code in the background, so the first export does not wait for a download.

## show a small preview of the crop

call \`exportCrop\` with a small \`maxSize\` in \`onLoad\` and in \`onValueCommit\`, and show the blob with \`URL.createObjectURL()\`. a photo nobody touches sends no commit, so \`onLoad\` draws the first preview. pass an \`AbortController\` \`signal\` and abort the last export on each commit, so a slow one never overwrites a newer one. revoke the old object url when a new preview replaces it:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

export const CropWithPreview = ({ file }: { file: File }) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const last = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
  const [preview, setPreview] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => () => last.current?.abort(), []);
  useEffect(
    () => () => {
      if (preview) URL.revokeObjectURL(preview);
    },
    [preview]
  );

  const refresh = async () => {
    if (!cropper.current) return;
    last.current?.abort();
    const controller = new AbortController();
    last.current = controller;
    try {
      const { blob } = await exportCrop(cropper.current, {
        maxSize: { width: 256, height: 256 },
        signal: controller.signal,
      });
      if (!controller.signal.aborted) {
        setPreview(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
      }
    } catch (error) {
      if (!controller.signal.aborted) console.error(error);
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root
        src={file}
        actionsRef={cropper}
        onLoad={() => void refresh()}
        onValueCommit={() => void refresh()}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      {preview && <img src={preview} alt="Crop preview" />}
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## cut the original on a server

call \`getPixelCrop()\` when you upload, because a photo the user never touches sends no commit. it returns the export plan in source pixels, or \`null\` until a photo loads. each commit also carries it as \`details.pixelCrop\`. send the whole plan with the file, because \`pixelRect\` is \`null\` with a straighten angle or perspective.

- \`width\` \`number\`

  the output width in pixels, never below 1 px. \`getPixelCrop()\` takes a scale, 1 by default and for anything but a positive number
- \`height\` \`number\`

  the output height in pixels at the same scale
- \`pixelRect\` \`PixelRect | null\`

  \`{ left, top, width, height }\` in the upright original, or \`null\` with a straighten angle or perspective
- \`rotateExpand\` \`{ flop, degrees, dx, dy }\`

  the mirror as \`flop\`, the quarter turns times 90 plus the straighten angle as \`degrees\`, and the crop's centre from the photo's centre after that whole turn as \`dx\` and \`dy\`, in source pixels
- \`forward\` \`AffineMatrix\`

  the map from source pixels to output pixels, as \`[a, b, c, d, e, f]\` in canvas \`setTransform()\` order. alone it holds only when \`homography\` is \`null\`. with perspective, \`output = forward(c + H(s - c))\`, with \`c\` the centre of the image in source pixels
- \`inverse\` \`AffineMatrix\`

  the map from output pixels back to source pixels. alone it holds only when \`homography\` is \`null\`. with perspective, \`s = c + H⁻¹(inverse(output) - c)\`
- \`homography\` \`Matrix3 | null\`

  the 3 by 3 perspective matrix \`H\`, row by row, or \`null\` with no perspective. divide by the third coordinate after you apply it

with no straighten and no perspective, the plan comes down to plain numbers that any tool can use: the rectangle, the flop and the turn.

\`toSharp\` from \`@zriz/core\` turns the plan into sharp steps. [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) shows the route for a level crop and a straightened one.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes faq as markdown 1`] = `
"# faq

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

short answers, each with a link to the page that covers it in full.

## setup

### what does zriz need

react 18.2 or newer, 19 included, from one build. both packages ship types, and \`@types/react\` 18.2 or newer is an optional peer. [quick start](https://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.md) has the install.

### which browsers does it run in

chrome, edge and firefox in their latest two versions, safari and ios safari 18 and newer, and chrome and samsung internet on android. a two-finger trackpad twist rotates only in safari. other browsers rotate with the dial and the keys.

### does it work with the next.js app router

yes. every part is a client component, so a server component can render them. callbacks such as \`onValueCommit\` are functions, so put them in a client file of your own.

### does it render on the server

yes. the server renders the parts with no photo, and the photo loads in the browser after hydration.

### does it work with vue or svelte

there are no vue or svelte parts. \`@zriz/core/dom\` binds a cropper to plain elements, so any framework can drive it. [vanilla js](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md) shows the setup.

### can i use my own button component

yes. pass it to \`render\` on any part, and zriz merges its props and ref into yours. [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md) has the code.

### why is my viewport empty

\`Viewport\` has no height of its own, so it starts at zero height. give it a height, as in [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md).

### can i test it with jsdom

jsdom has no \`ResizeObserver\`, so \`Viewport\` and \`DialTicks\` throw when they mount. stub it in your test setup. to test crop logic with no dom at all, use \`createCropper\` with a manual clock, as [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) shows.

\`\`\`ts
globalThis.ResizeObserver ??= class {
  observe() {}
  unobserve() {}
  disconnect() {}
};
\`\`\`

## photos and export

### can it open heic photos from an iphone

safari decodes heic, so iphone photos open there. in other browsers the load fails with \`unsupported-format\`. to open them everywhere, call \`loadImage\` yourself with a heic decoder as \`decode\`, as [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) shows.

### how do i get the cropped file

call \`exportCrop\` from \`@zriz/core/dom\` with the cropper from \`actionsRef\`. it resolves with a \`Blob\` and its width and height. [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) has the code.

### can i crop on the server

yes. send the original file and the plan from \`getPixelCrop()\`, then turn that plan into sharp calls with \`toSharp\`. [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) has the route.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes full-editor as markdown 1`] = `
"# full editor

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

the crop area, the dial, zoom, aspect ratios, the buttons and a jpeg export in one component.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/full-editor.

## lay out the crop area

give the viewport a size. the stylesheet paints the mask, the crop frame, the handles and the grid. eight \`Handle\` parts let the visitor drag every corner and edge.

\`\`\`tsx
const handles = [
  "top-left",
  "top",
  "top-right",
  "right",
  "bottom-right",
  "bottom",
  "bottom-left",
  "left",
] as const;

<Cropper.Viewport
  style={{ width: 640, height: 480, padding: 24 }}
>
  <Cropper.Image />
  <Cropper.Mask />
  <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
    <Cropper.Grid />
    {handles.map((position) => (
      <Cropper.Handle
        key={position}
        position={position}
      />
    ))}
  </Cropper.Area>
</Cropper.Viewport>
\`\`\`

## straighten with the dial

the dial fills its parent unless you give it a width, see [dial](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md).

\`DialValue\` prints the angle as a whole number. \`format\` adds the degree sign.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.DialValue
  format={(degrees) => \`\${Math.round(degrees)}°\`}
/>
<Cropper.Dial
  aria-label="Straighten"
  style={{ width: 361 }}
>
  <Cropper.DialTicks />
</Cropper.Dial>
\`\`\`

## add a zoom slider

zriz ships \`Zoom\` and \`ZoomThumb\` with no paint. \`track\` draws a 2 px line and \`thumb\` draws the knob. zriz moves the knob from the left end at 1× to the right end at the largest zoom.

\`\`\`tsx
const track = {
  width: 200,
  height: 24,
  backgroundImage:
    "linear-gradient(currentColor, currentColor)",
  backgroundSize: "100% 2px",
  backgroundPosition: "center",
  backgroundRepeat: "no-repeat",
};

const thumb = {
  top: 4,
  width: 16,
  height: 16,
  borderRadius: 8,
  background: "currentColor",
  translate: "-50%",
};

<Cropper.Zoom aria-label="Zoom" style={track}>
  <Cropper.ZoomThumb style={thumb} />
</Cropper.Zoom>
\`\`\`

## pick an aspect ratio

\`AspectGroup\` is one tab stop. [aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md) lists its keys.

\`Orientation\` swaps 4:5 for 5:4 and 16:9 for 9:16. it is off while the aspect is free, original or square. after a swap no item is checked, because the list has neither 5:4 nor 9:16. [aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md) flips the labels with the orientation so one item stays checked.

\`\`\`tsx
const aspects = [
  { label: "free", value: "free" },
  { label: "original", value: "original" },
  { label: "square", value: 1 },
  { label: "4:5", value: 4 / 5 },
  { label: "16:9", value: 16 / 9 },
] as const;

<Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
  {aspects.map((aspect) => (
    <Cropper.AspectItem
      key={aspect.label}
      value={aspect.value}
    >
      {aspect.label}
    </Cropper.AspectItem>
  ))}
</Cropper.AspectGroup>
<Cropper.Orientation>orientation</Cropper.Orientation>
\`\`\`

## turn, flip and reset

the buttons come with no paint, so put your own icons or words inside. \`Rotate\` turns the photo 90° counter-clockwise, \`Flip\` mirrors it, and \`Reset\` puts the crop back where it started. \`Reset\` stays hidden until the crop changes.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Rotate>rotate</Cropper.Rotate>
<Cropper.Flip>flip</Cropper.Flip>
<Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
\`\`\`

## describe it to screen readers

\`Instructions\` is hidden text. the crop area points to it with \`aria-describedby\`, so a screen reader reads it when the area gets focus.

the dial and the zoom slider read out their own values. the buttons do not, so \`announce\` speaks for them after each commit. it returns \`null\` for any other change. [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) covers the keys and the labels.

\`\`\`tsx
const spoken: Partial<Record<ChangeReason, string>> = {
  "rotate-90": "Turned 90 degrees",
  flip: "Flipped",
  orientation: "Orientation swapped",
  reset: "Crop reset",
};

const announce = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) =>
  spoken[details.reason] ?? null;

const instructions = [
  "Drag the photo to move it.",
  "Arrows move the crop. Alt and arrows resize it.",
  "Plus and minus zoom, and 0 resets.",
].join(" ");

<Cropper.Instructions>
  {instructions}
</Cropper.Instructions>
<Cropper.Announcer announce={announce} />
\`\`\`

## export a jpeg

\`exportCrop\` makes a jpeg unless the crop is a circle. the button stays off until \`onLoad\` fires, because an export before that rejects. \`exportCrop\` comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`. add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md).

an export can still fail on a very large photo, so \`save\` shows its own alert when it does. \`onError\` shows a second one when the photo does not open. [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists the codes.

\`key={src}\` gives each photo a fresh cropper, and the check on \`shown\` turns the button off and hides both alerts when \`src\` changes.

\`\`\`tsx
const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);
const [exportFailed, setExportFailed] = useState(false);
const [shown, setShown] = useState(src);
if (shown !== src) {
  setShown(src);
  setReady(false);
  setFailed(false);
  setExportFailed(false);
}

const save = async () => {
  if (!actions.current) return;
  setExportFailed(false);
  try {
    const { blob } = await exportCrop(actions.current);
    onSave(blob);
  } catch {
    setExportFailed(true);
  }
};

<Cropper.Root
  key={src}
  actionsRef={actions}
  src={src}
  onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
  onError={() => setFailed(true)}
>

<button type="button" disabled={!ready} onClick={save}>
  export
</button>

{failed && <p role="alert">this photo does not open.</p>}

{exportFailed && (
  <p role="alert">this crop did not save. try again.</p>
)}
\`\`\`

## the whole file

\`Editor\` takes a photo url and hands \`onSave\` the jpeg:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type { ChangeReason } from "@zriz/core";
import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type {
  CommitDetails,
  Cropper as CropperApi,
  CropValue,
} from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

const handles = [
  "top-left",
  "top",
  "top-right",
  "right",
  "bottom-right",
  "bottom",
  "bottom-left",
  "left",
] as const;

const track = {
  width: 200,
  height: 24,
  backgroundImage:
    "linear-gradient(currentColor, currentColor)",
  backgroundSize: "100% 2px",
  backgroundPosition: "center",
  backgroundRepeat: "no-repeat",
};

const thumb = {
  top: 4,
  width: 16,
  height: 16,
  borderRadius: 8,
  background: "currentColor",
  translate: "-50%",
};

const aspects = [
  { label: "free", value: "free" },
  { label: "original", value: "original" },
  { label: "square", value: 1 },
  { label: "4:5", value: 4 / 5 },
  { label: "16:9", value: 16 / 9 },
] as const;

const spoken: Partial<Record<ChangeReason, string>> = {
  "rotate-90": "Turned 90 degrees",
  flip: "Flipped",
  orientation: "Orientation swapped",
  reset: "Crop reset",
};

const announce = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) =>
  spoken[details.reason] ?? null;

const instructions = [
  "Drag the photo to move it.",
  "Arrows move the crop. Alt and arrows resize it.",
  "Plus and minus zoom, and 0 resets.",
].join(" ");

type Props = { src: string; onSave: (jpeg: Blob) => void };

export const Editor = ({ src, onSave }: Props) => {
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);
  const [exportFailed, setExportFailed] = useState(false);
  const [shown, setShown] = useState(src);
  if (shown !== src) {
    setShown(src);
    setReady(false);
    setFailed(false);
    setExportFailed(false);
  }

  const save = async () => {
    if (!actions.current) return;
    setExportFailed(false);
    try {
      const { blob } = await exportCrop(actions.current);
      onSave(blob);
    } catch {
      setExportFailed(true);
    }
  };

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      key={src}
      actionsRef={actions}
      src={src}
      onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
      onError={() => setFailed(true)}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport
        style={{ width: 640, height: 480, padding: 24 }}
      >
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
          <Cropper.Grid />
          {handles.map((position) => (
            <Cropper.Handle
              key={position}
              position={position}
            />
          ))}
        </Cropper.Area>
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.DialValue
        format={(degrees) => \`\${Math.round(degrees)}°\`}
      />
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ width: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
      <Cropper.Zoom aria-label="Zoom" style={track}>
        <Cropper.ZoomThumb style={thumb} />
      </Cropper.Zoom>
      <Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
        {aspects.map((aspect) => (
          <Cropper.AspectItem
            key={aspect.label}
            value={aspect.value}
          >
            {aspect.label}
          </Cropper.AspectItem>
        ))}
      </Cropper.AspectGroup>
      <Cropper.Orientation>orientation</Cropper.Orientation>
      <Cropper.Rotate>rotate</Cropper.Rotate>
      <Cropper.Flip>flip</Cropper.Flip>
      <Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
      <Cropper.Instructions>
        {instructions}
      </Cropper.Instructions>
      <Cropper.Announcer announce={announce} />
      <button type="button" disabled={!ready} onClick={save}>
        export
      </button>
      {failed && <p role="alert">this photo does not open.</p>}
      {exportFailed && (
        <p role="alert">this crop did not save. try again.</p>
      )}
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`
"
`;

exports[`content > writes hooks as markdown 1`] = `
"# hooks

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

seven hooks from \`@zriz/react\` that read the cropper and build parts of your own.

call a hook in any component inside \`Root\`. this label reads \`edited\` once the crop changes:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperState } from "@zriz/react";

export const Status = () => {
  const pristine = useCropperState((s) => s.pristine);
  return <span>{pristine ? "original" : "edited"}</span>;
};
\`\`\`

outside \`Root\`, pass the cropper yourself. it goes last in \`useCropperState\`, \`useCropperValue\` and \`useCropperFrame\`, and in the options of \`useZoom\`. without it, they throw the error listed on [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md).

## call methods from your own buttons

each \`Root\` runs one cropper. pass a ref to \`actionsRef\` to reach it once \`Root\` has mounted. the ref stays set while a suspense boundary shows its fallback, and is \`null\` while \`<Activity>\` hides \`Root\`. when \`Root\` unmounts, the ref clears with the passive effects, so a parent's \`useLayoutEffect\` in that commit still reads the cropper. [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) lists its methods.

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useRef } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const save = () => {
    console.log(cropper.current?.getPixelCrop());
  };
  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root src={src} actionsRef={cropper}>
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button onClick={save}>save</button>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## the hooks

### \`useCropper\`

\`useCropper(options?)\` creates the cropper in a component above \`Root\`. use it when that component needs the cropper during render. hand the cropper to \`Root\` and to any control:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { attachImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { useCropper } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const cropper = useCropper({ maxZoom: 8 });
  useEffect(() => () => attachImage(cropper, null), [cropper]);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => cropper.reset()}>reset</button>
      <Cropper.Root cropper={cropper} src={src}>
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

it takes the \`createCropper\` options on [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) except \`clock\`, plus the controlled \`aspect\`, \`rotation\` and \`zoom\`, and \`cropper\` to reuse one you already made. \`disabled\`, \`maxZoom\`, \`minSize\`, \`value\` and the two callbacks follow later renders. the cropper reads \`defaultValue\`, \`image\`, \`focalLength\` and \`plugins\` once, when it is made.

when \`Root\` gets \`cropper\`, it ignores its own \`defaultValue\`, \`image\`, \`focalLength\` and \`plugins\`, because the cropper already exists. give each option to \`useCropper\` or to \`Root\`, never to both.

\`Root\` frees the photo only for a cropper it made. free a \`useCropper\` cropper's photo yourself when the component unmounts, with \`attachImage(cropper, null)\` from \`@zriz/core/dom\`, as the snippet does. add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md).

\`Root\` keeps its photo while \`<Activity>\` hides it. react runs no cleanup when it removes a tree that is hidden, so \`Root\` cannot free the photo then. to remove a hidden \`Root\`, make the cropper with \`useCropper\` above it and call \`attachImage(cropper, null)\` yourself.

pass one cropper to one \`Root\`. \`shape\`, \`motion\` and \`recentreDelay\` belong to the cropper, so a second \`Root\` overwrites them, even with its defaults. keep \`Instructions\` in the \`Root\` that holds \`Area\`. your own parts and hooks can reach the cropper through \`CropperContext\` or the \`cropper\` argument.

### \`useCropperState\`

\`useCropperState(selector, cropper?)\` returns the part of the snapshot you select and renders again when it changes. the snapshot changes when a state flips, never on every frame.

- \`ready\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` once a photo is loaded and sized
- \`interacting\` \`ChangeReason | null\`

  names the gesture in progress
- \`settling\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` while any animation runs: a thrown dial, the recentre, a rotate or flip
- \`pristine\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` while the crop matches the reset value
- \`disabled\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` while the cropper refuses gestures and most writes, see [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md)
- \`aspect\` \`Aspect\`

  holds the current aspect
- \`quarterTurns\` \`0 | 1 | 2 | 3\`

  holds the current quarter turns
- \`mirrored\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` while the photo is flipped
- \`committed\` \`CropValue\`

  holds the last committed value

return one field or a primitive. a selector that builds a new object or array on every call makes react render forever.

### \`useCropperValue\`

\`useCropperValue(cropper?)\` returns the last committed value and renders once per commit. a new \`value\` prop and writes with \`emit: false\` change it without a commit, so the hook does not render for them. the first fit when a photo loads does not commit either, but the hook renders when a photo arrives or leaves, so it shows that fit. the fields are on [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md).

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperValue } from "@zriz/react";

export const Angle = () => {
  const value = useCropperValue();
  return <p>{value.rotation.toFixed(1)}°</p>;
};
\`\`\`

### \`useCropperFrame\`

\`useCropperFrame(listener, cropper?)\` calls the listener on every frame that moved, without a render. the frame holds \`time\`, \`value\` and \`state\`, the crop in image pixels, which is \`null\` before a photo loads. write the live angle straight into the dom:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperFrame } from "@zriz/react";
import { useRef } from "react";

export const LiveAngle = () => {
  const label = useRef<HTMLOutputElement>(null);
  useCropperFrame(({ value }) => {
    const angle = value.rotation.toFixed(1);
    if (label.current) label.current.textContent = \`\${angle}°\`;
  });
  return <output ref={label} />;
};
\`\`\`

### \`useZoom\`

\`useZoom(options?)\` connects a slider of your own to the zoom. each drag counts as one \`zoom-control\` gesture on [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md). it takes two options, \`max\` and \`cropper\`. [zoom](https://zriz.dev/docs/zoom.md) builds a range input with it. it returns:

- \`value\` \`number\`

  holds the zoom rounded to 3 decimals. it updates every frame
- \`min\` \`number\`

  is always 1, the largest crop
- \`max\` \`number\`

  is the \`max\` option, or the cropper's \`maxZoom\`, 15 by default. \`max\` sets the slider's range only. the cropper still stops at \`maxZoom\`
- \`dragging\` \`boolean\`

  is \`true\` while a drag runs
- \`onValueChange\` \`(zoom) => void\`

  starts the gesture on the first call and sets the zoom
- \`onValueCommitted\` \`() => void\`

  ends the gesture. call it when the slider lets go

### \`useCoverScale\`

\`useCoverScale({ image, crop, rotation })\` returns the smallest scale that keeps a straightened photo free of empty corners, for a crop centred on it. it calls no react hooks, so it is safe in loops and conditions. outside react, \`coverScale(image, crop, radians)\` from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md), does the same.

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCoverScale } from "@zriz/react";

export const Scale = () => {
  const scale = useCoverScale({
    image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
    crop: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
    rotation: 5,
  });
  return <p>{scale.toFixed(4)}</p>;
};
\`\`\`

a full-frame crop of a 4032 × 3024 photo, straightened 5°, needs 1.1124. the photo grows 11.2%.

### \`useRender\`

\`useRender(options)\` renders an element the way every zriz part does. use it to build a part of your own. this is the label from the top of the page as a part:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperState, useRender } from "@zriz/react";
import type { ClassName, RenderProp } from "@zriz/react";

interface EditedState {
  pristine: boolean;
}

interface EditedProps {
  render?: RenderProp<EditedState>;
  className?: ClassName<EditedState>;
}

export const Edited = ({ render, className }: EditedProps) => {
  const pristine = useCropperState((s) => s.pristine);
  return useRender({
    tag: "span",
    state: { pristine },
    render,
    className,
    props: { children: pristine ? "original" : "edited" },
  });
};
\`\`\`

your part follows the \`render\` and \`className\` rules on [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). it takes:

- \`tag\` \`keyof JSX.IntrinsicElements\` required

  sets the tag, for example \`"span"\`
- \`state\` \`S\` required

  goes to \`render\` and \`className\` functions
- \`props\` \`object\` required

  sets the element's props. take \`render\` and your own props out first, as the parts do
- \`ref\` \`Ref<Element>\`

  attaches to the element
- \`render\` \`RenderProp<S>\`

  replaces the element, as on every part
- \`className\` \`ClassName<S>\`

  sets the class: a string, or a function of state
"
`;

exports[`content > writes introduction as markdown 1`] = `
"# introduction

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

zriz is an image cropper for react with a straighten dial, like the crop tool in ios photos.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/introduction.

## what it does

| feature | what you get |
| - | - |
| [straighten](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md) | a dial turns the photo up to 45° each way and glides on after a flick. the photo zooms in by the least amount that keeps every corner of the crop filled |
| [move and zoom](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md) | drag, pinch, trackpad and wheel. pull past an edge and the photo stretches, then springs back when you let go |
| [rotate and flip](https://zriz.dev/docs/buttons.md) | animated quarter turns and mirroring, and a reset that undoes every change |
| [aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md) | free, the photo's own ratio or any ratio you name, with a portrait and landscape swap |
| [circle crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/avatar.md) | a round mask for avatars, exported as a png with transparent corners |
| [export](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) | a file made off the main thread, or a plan your server uses to cut the original with sharp |
| [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) | keys on every control, labels and announcements in your own words, and shorter motion when the system asks |
| [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) | an optional stylesheet, css variables, a data attribute for every state, dark mode and tailwind |
| [plain data](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) | the crop is a small object you can save, restore, undo or send |
| [without react](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md) | the same core binds to plain elements, so it runs in any framework |

## build it from parts

import the parts as one namespace from \`@zriz/react/cropper\`, and the hooks from \`@zriz/react\`. these pages call the parts by their short names, such as \`Root\`.

each part renders one outer element, which takes your props, \`className\` and ref. \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Area\` go inside \`Viewport\`. the dial, zoom and buttons can sit anywhere inside \`Root\`, so your layout decides where they go:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo} onValueCommit={save}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 400, height: 300 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
  <Cropper.DialValue />
  <Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
    <Cropper.DialTicks />
  </Cropper.Dial>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

\`onValueCommit\` hands you the crop each time a change ends.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes loading-images as markdown 1`] = `
"# loading images

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

open a photo from a file input, a url or a blob, and check which formats and sizes load.

## open a file from an input

\`src\` on \`Root\` takes a url string, a \`Blob\`, a \`File\`, or \`null\` for an empty cropper. keep the picked file in state and pass it straight to \`src\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<input
  type="file"
  accept="image/*"
  onChange={(event) =>
    setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null)
  }
/>
<Cropper.Root src={file}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

zriz reads the format from the first 4096 bytes of the file, so a wrong name or mime type does not stop a photo from opening. when \`src\` changes, zriz cancels a load that is still running and keeps \`onError\` quiet for it. the previous photo stays on screen until the new one is ready, and stays there when the new one fails.

## start each photo from the full frame

a new \`src\` keeps the current crop, angle and flip, and fits them to the new photo. to start every photo from the whole frame, give \`Root\` a \`key\` that changes with each pick, such as a counter you bump in \`onChange\`. a new \`key\` also frees the previous photo at once.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root key={picks} src={file}>
  {children}
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## open a url on another domain

zriz fetches a url with \`fetch\` in cors mode, so the server that hosts the photo must send \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`. without it, the load fails with \`cross-origin\`. a 404 or any other error status fails with \`decode\`.

if that server needs cookies, pass \`crossOrigin="use-credentials"\`. the server then has to name your origin in \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`, not \`*\`, and send \`Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\`.

if that server wants a token in a header, fetch the photo yourself with that header and pass the response's blob to \`src\`.

## check which formats open

| format | what happens |
| - | - |
| jpeg, png, webp, avif, gif | open in every current browser |
| bmp | opens in every current browser, but safari cannot open some bmp files with a v4 or v5 header. those fail with \`decode\`. pass a \`decode\` function to open them, or convert the file to jpeg first |
| heic, jpeg xl, tiff | open where the browser decodes them. they fail with \`unsupported-format\` elsewhere, and when the file is damaged |
| svg | takes its size from \`width\` and \`height\` in px, in, cm, mm, q, pt or pc, or from \`viewBox\`. without both it opens at 300 by 150. zriz draws it once at that size, at most the preview size, and exports from that drawing |
| animated gif, png, webp, avif | open on their first frame. \`animated\` on the loaded image is \`true\`, and always for a gif. an \`img\` already playing opens on the frame it shows in safari, so pass a \`Blob\` for the first frame |

every exif orientation opens upright. zriz measures the crop value and the export plan in the upright photo.

## handle large photos

a worker decodes a preview sized for the screen. the original stays a blob until you export.

| limit | size |
| - | - |
| preview size | twice the screen's long edge in device pixels, at most 4096 px on a touch screen and 8192 px elsewhere |
| export size | 16.8 megapixels on a touch screen and 268 megapixels elsewhere, with a long edge of at most 16383 px for webp and 65500 px for other types. a bigger crop comes out scaled down |

the fetch and the decode share one time limit. past it the load fails with \`timeout\`, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) gives the number. zriz runs three decodes at a time, and a photo waiting for its turn is not on the clock. photos that load together share the network, so a slow link can push all of them past the limit. to allow more time, load the photo yourself and pass \`timeout\`, as in the heic section below.

a content security policy needs these sources:

| directive | what it enables |
| - | - |
| \`worker-src blob:\` | decoding and exporting off the main thread. without it zriz does both on the main thread, and the page can stutter while a large photo opens |
| \`img-src blob:\` | the moving layer during gestures and display-p3 export. without it the photo paints on the canvas during gestures, a crop with perspective stays empty, and a display-p3 export fails with \`decode\` |
| \`img-src data:\` | svg files. without it an svg fails with \`decode\` |
| \`connect-src\` | each host you load photos from, plus \`blob:\` and \`data:\` if you pass those urls as \`src\`. without it the load fails with \`cross-origin\` |

## open heic on every browser

to decode a format the browser cannot, call \`loadImage\` yourself and pass a \`decode\` function. zriz calls it only when the browser's own decode fails. it gets the \`Blob\` and returns an \`ImageBitmap\`. save this as \`decoder.ts\`. its \`convert\` sends the file to a route on your server that turns it into jpeg. \`loadImage\` and \`attachImage\` come from \`@zriz/core/dom\`, so add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md):

\`\`\`ts
import { attachImage, loadImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper } from "@zriz/react";

const convert = async (blob: Blob) => {
  const response = await fetch("/api/convert", {
    method: "POST",
    body: blob,
  });
  return await createImageBitmap(await response.blob());
};

export const open = async (
  cropper: Cropper,
  file: File,
  signal: AbortSignal
) => {
  const image = await loadImage(file, {
    timeout: 30_000,
    decode: convert,
    signal,
  });
  attachImage(cropper, image);
};
\`\`\`

pass a ref to \`actionsRef\`, leave \`src\` unset on \`Root\` and call \`open()\` from the file input. \`onLoad\` and \`onError\` do not fire for a photo you attach yourself, so the component tracks both from \`open()\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import { open } from "./decoder";

type Props = { onSave: (cropper: CropperApi) => void };

export const Heic = ({ onSave }: Props) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const last = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);

  return (
    <>
      <input
        type="file"
        accept="image/*,.heic"
        onChange={(event) => {
          const file = event.target.files?.[0];
          if (!file || !cropper.current) return;
          last.current?.abort();
          const controller = new AbortController();
          last.current = controller;
          setReady(false);
          setFailed(false);
          open(cropper.current, file, controller.signal).then(
            () => setReady(true),
            () => {
              if (!controller.signal.aborted) setFailed(true);
            }
          );
        }}
      />
      <Cropper.Root actionsRef={cropper}>
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={!ready}
        onClick={() =>
          cropper.current && onSave(cropper.current)
        }
      >
        save
      </button>
      {failed && (
        <p role="alert">this photo does not open here.</p>
      )}
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`ready\` turns the save button on once the photo is attached. \`onSave\` gets the cropper for \`exportCrop\`, as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). a new pick aborts the load still running, so a slow file never replaces a newer one.

\`loadImage\` also takes \`timeout\` in milliseconds, \`previewEdge\`, the longest side of the preview in pixels, \`crossOrigin\` and an abort \`signal\`. it accepts an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, \`OffscreenCanvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` as its source. a \`video\` needs a current frame, so wait for \`loadeddata\` or \`seeked\`, or the load fails with \`decode\`. a frame with nothing opaque in it counts as no frame.

\`attachImage\` frees a photo when it leaves its last cropper, and \`destroy()\` does the same for the photo on that cropper. a freed photo cannot be attached again, so load it again.

## handle a failed load

\`onError\` on \`Root\` gets a \`ZrizError\`. its \`code\` names the problem, such as \`timeout\` or \`cross-origin\`. \`onLoad\` fires instead when the photo is ready, with its \`width\`, \`height\` and \`mime\`. [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists every code with its fix.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes playground as markdown 1`] = `
"# playground

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

change the main props on a live cropper and copy the code that matches what you see.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/playground.

## change the props

### \`Root\`

- \`shape\`: \`rect\`, \`circle\`
- \`motion\`: \`auto\`, \`always\`, \`reduce\`
- \`dir\`: \`ltr\`, \`rtl\`
- \`maxZoom\`: from 1× to 15×
- \`recentreDelay\`: from 0 ms to 2000 ms
- \`disabled\`: on or off

### \`Viewport\`

- \`wheel\`: \`pan\`, \`zoom\`, \`none\`
- \`rotateGesture\`: on or off

## tilt the photo

perspective tilts the photo as if the camera leaned. no part sets it, so pass it in \`value\` or \`defaultValue\`, or call \`setValue()\`. [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) has its range.

- \`vertical\`: from -30° to 30°
- \`horizontal\`: from -30° to 30°

## copy the code

the code builds the cropper in the demo, with only the props you changed from their defaults:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={photo}
  motion="reduce"
  maxZoom={8}
  onValueCommit={save}
>
  <Cropper.Viewport wheel="zoom" style={{ height: 400 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
      <Cropper.Grid />
    </Cropper.Area>
  </Cropper.Viewport>
  <Cropper.DialValue
    format={(angle) => \`\${Math.round(angle)}°\`}
  />
  <Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
    <Cropper.DialTicks />
  </Cropper.Dial>
  <Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
    <Cropper.AspectItem value="free">free</Cropper.AspectItem>
    <Cropper.AspectItem value={1}>1:1</Cropper.AspectItem>
    <Cropper.AspectItem value={4 / 5}>4:5</Cropper.AspectItem>
    <Cropper.AspectItem value={16 / 9}>16:9</Cropper.AspectItem>
  </Cropper.AspectGroup>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## watch the value

the crop as you move it, the same object \`onValueChange\` and \`onValueCommit\` hand you:

\`\`\`ts
const value = {
  center: { x: 0.52, y: 0.47 },
  size: { width: 0.81, height: 0.81 },
  rotation: 4,
  quarterTurns: 0,
  mirrored: false,
  perspective: { vertical: 0, horizontal: 0 },
  aspect: "free",
};
\`\`\`

## watch the events

each row is a reason and a phase from \`onValueChange\`, or a commit from \`onValueCommit\`, as in [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md), newest first:

\`\`\`text
12:04:31  dial commit 4°
12:04:31  dial end
12:04:30  dial move
12:04:29  dial start
\`\`\`

## export the crop

download the crop as a png made by \`exportCrop\` with \`type: "image/png"\`. with \`shape\` set to \`"circle"\` above, the corners come out transparent.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes quick-start as markdown 1`] = `
"# quick start

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

go from an empty project to a cropper that hands you the crop.

## set it up with an agent

paste this prompt into claude code, cursor or any other coding agent. it reads this page and does the steps below for you:

\`\`\`text
add zriz to this project. read https://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.md and follow it: install @zriz/react, import its stylesheet and render a cropper with corner handles, the straighten dial and a file picker for the photo. build it from the zriz parts, not a crop box of your own, and give every part that asks for one an aria-label. when it works, show me where onValueCommit hands back the crop.
\`\`\`

## install

\`\`\`sh
pnpm add @zriz/react
\`\`\`

npm, yarn and bun work too. [faq](https://zriz.dev/docs/faq.md) lists what zriz needs.

## render a cropper

paste this component into a page. it loads a sample photo, and the stylesheet gives it the default look:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";

const photo = "https://picsum.photos/id/1015/1600/1200";

export const PhotoCropper = () => {
  return (
    <Cropper.Root src={photo}>
      <Cropper.Viewport
        style={{ width: 400, height: 300, padding: 24 }}
      >
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
          <Cropper.Handle position="top-left" />
          <Cropper.Handle position="top-right" />
          <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-right" />
          <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-left" />
        </Cropper.Area>
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ width: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`Viewport\` needs a size, and the image and the mask fill it. the 24 px padding leaves room for the handles, see [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md). the cropper now drags, pinches, resizes and straightens:

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.

## let the user pick a photo

keep the chosen file in state and pass it to \`src\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useState } from "react";

export const PhotoCropper = () => {
  const [photo, setPhoto] = useState<File | null>(null);
  return (
    <>
      <input
        type="file"
        accept="image/*"
        onChange={(e) => setPhoto(e.target.files?.[0] ?? null)}
      />
      <Cropper.Root
        src={photo}
        onError={(error) => console.error(error.code)}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ width: 400, height: 300, padding: 24 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
            <Cropper.Handle position="top-left" />
            <Cropper.Handle position="top-right" />
            <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-right" />
            <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-left" />
          </Cropper.Area>
        </Cropper.Viewport>
        <Cropper.Dial
          aria-label="Straighten"
          style={{ width: 361 }}
        >
          <Cropper.DialTicks />
        </Cropper.Dial>
      </Cropper.Root>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`src\` takes a url, a \`File\` or a \`Blob\`. while it is \`null\`, the viewport stays empty. when a photo cannot load, \`onError\` gets a \`ZrizError\`, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists its codes. a url from another domain needs cors headers, and a new photo keeps the current crop. [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) explains both.

## get the crop

add \`onValueCommit\` to \`Root\` in the picker above. it fires once when a gesture ends, a key press on the crop or the dial included, or on the frame after a write such as \`rotate90()\`. a photo the user never touches sends no commit, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md).

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={photo}
  onError={(error) => console.error(error.code)}
  onValueCommit={(value, details) => {
    console.log(value, details.pixelCrop);
  }}
>
\`\`\`

\`value\` is the crop as fractions of the photo, ready to store and load back. [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) lists every field. \`details.pixelCrop\` is the same crop in source pixels. \`getPixelCrop()\` on the cropper from \`actionsRef\` reads it for an untouched photo, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md). [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) turns the crop into a file in the browser or on your server.

[playground](https://zriz.dev/docs/playground.md) changes the main props live, and [full editor](https://zriz.dev/docs/full-editor.md) puts every part in one component.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes root as markdown 1`] = `
"# root

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Root\` loads the photo, keeps the crop and holds every other part.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/root.

## anatomy

everything goes inside \`Root\`. \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Area\` go in \`Viewport\`. the controls can sit anywhere, in any wrapper:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
      <Cropper.Grid />
    </Cropper.Area>
  </Cropper.Viewport>
  <Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
    <Cropper.DialTicks />
  </Cropper.Dial>
  <div className="toolbar">
    <Cropper.Rotate>rotate</Cropper.Rotate>
    <Cropper.Flip>flip</Cropper.Flip>
    <Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
  </div>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## lock the cropper while you save

\`disabled\` ignores every gesture, key and button press, and every cropper method that moves the crop, such as \`rotate90()\` or \`setZoom()\`, except \`setValue()\`, \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\`. \`Root\` gets \`data-disabled\`, so css can grey it out. \`value\` and a new \`src\` still apply, and \`aspect\`, \`rotation\` and \`zoom\` set while disabled apply once it ends:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo} disabled={saving}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

grey out the buttons while it is set:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="root"][data-disabled] button {
  opacity: 0.4;
  pointer-events: none;
}
\`\`\`

## dim the controls during a gesture

\`Root\` carries data attributes for what the cropper is doing. this rule fades the toolbar during any gesture, a key press included, and for a short hold after it, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="root"][data-interacting] .toolbar {
  opacity: 0.4;
}
\`\`\`

## api

### \`Root\`

renders a \`<div>\`. every other part goes inside it. every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ snapshot, chrome }\`. \`snapshot\` holds the fields \`useCropperState\` lists on [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md). \`chrome\` holds \`grid\`, \`active\`, \`transition\` and \`overscroll\`, the values behind \`data-grid\`, \`data-interacting\`, \`data-transition\` and \`data-overscroll\`.

- \`src\` \`string | Blob | null\`

  the photo: a url or a \`Blob\` such as a \`File\`. \`null\` clears it, \`undefined\` leaves it alone
- \`crossOrigin\` \`"anonymous" | "use-credentials"\`

  the cors mode when the photo is on another domain
- \`image\` \`Size | null\`

  the photo's pixel size up front, read once
- \`value\` \`CropValue\`

  controls the whole value. \`onValueChange\` does not fire when you set it
- \`defaultValue\` \`Partial<CropValue>\`

  the starting value. fields you leave out use the defaults
- \`aspect\` \`"free" | "original" | number\`

  controls the aspect alone, a ratio like \`4 / 3\`
- \`rotation\` \`number\`

  controls the straighten angle alone, up to 45° each way
- \`zoom\` \`number\`

  controls the zoom alone. 1 is the largest crop
- \`shape\` \`"rect" | "circle"\` = \`"rect"\`

  the crop shape. a circle locks the aspect to 1, draws a round mask and exports a round png, and the stylesheet hides the corner handles, see [avatar](https://zriz.dev/docs/avatar.md)
- \`minSize\` \`Size\` = \`{ width: 1, height: 1 }\`

  the smallest crop in source pixels. a large one narrows the straighten range
- \`maxZoom\` \`number\` = \`15\`

  the deepest zoom. every gesture, key and control stops there
- \`onValueChange\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  at most once per frame while the crop moves, with a reason and \`cancel()\`, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md)
- \`onValueCommit\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  called once when a gesture or a write is done, with \`pixelCrop\`, the export plan, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md)
- \`onLoad\` \`(image) => void\`

  called when the photo is decoded and ready to crop
- \`onError\` \`(error) => void\`

  called with a typed error when the photo cannot load, see [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md)
- \`cropper\` \`Cropper\`

  uses an instance from \`useCropper\`. \`image\`, \`defaultValue\`, \`plugins\` and \`focalLength\` then come from \`useCropper\`
- \`actionsRef\` \`Ref<Cropper>\`

  gives you the cropper, for calls like \`rotate90()\` from a button
- \`plugins\` \`readonly CropperPlugin[]\`

  runs each plugin's \`init\` once, when \`Root\` makes the cropper, see [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md)
- \`disabled\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  ignores gestures, keys, buttons and the cropper methods that move the crop. \`value\` and \`setValue()\` still apply. sets \`data-disabled\`
- \`motion\` \`"auto" | "always" | "reduce"\` = \`"auto"\`

  follows the system motion setting, or forces full or short motion
- \`recentreDelay\` \`number\` = \`500\`

  milliseconds after a gesture before the crop recentres
- \`dir\` \`"ltr" | "rtl"\` = \`"ltr"\`

  the reading direction for keys and sliders. \`Root\` also sets the html \`dir\` attribute
- \`locale\` \`string\`

  formats the values screen readers hear
- \`focalLength\` \`number\` = \`26\`

  the lens as a 35 mm equivalent, used for perspective correction. at a strong tilt, zriz lengthens a lens too short to keep the photo in front of it

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-interacting\` | during a gesture and for a short hold after it, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) |
| \`data-panning\` | while a pointer drags the photo |
| \`data-pinching\` | while two fingers pinch or twist on a touch screen, or a trackpad pinches in safari |
| \`data-resizing\` | while a handle resizes the crop |
| \`data-rotating\` | while a pointer drags the dial and while it glides after |
| \`data-zooming\` | while the wheel pans or zooms, a pointer drags \`Zoom\`, or a \`useZoom\` slider moves |
| \`data-settling\` | while any animation runs: a glide, a spring back, a recentre or a button's move |
| \`data-changed\` | while \`Reset\` has a change to undo |
| \`data-mirrored\` | while the photo is flipped |
| \`data-overscroll\` | while you pull past an edge or a zoom limit |
| \`data-disabled\` | while \`disabled\` is set |
| \`data-reduced-motion\` | while short motion is on |
| \`data-orientation\` | \`"landscape"\` above 1, \`"portrait"\` below 1, \`"square"\` at 1 and while the aspect is \`"free"\` or \`"original"\` |
| \`data-grid\` | \`"coarse"\`, \`"fine"\` or \`"none"\`, see [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md) |
| \`data-transition\` | \`"rotate"\`, \`"flip"\`, \`"morph"\` or \`"recentre"\` while that transition runs. \`"morph"\` covers reset, an aspect pick, an orientation swap and undo |
| \`data-shape\` | \`"rect"\` or \`"circle"\` |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes saving-state as markdown 1`] = `
"# saving state

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

keep the crop for each photo in local storage and bring it back on the next visit.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/saving-state.

## read the saved crop before the cropper mounts

each photo gets its own key, built from an \`id\` you pass, such as the photo's id in your database. \`parse\` turns the stored string back into a crop, or returns \`null\` when there is none. [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) shows what it does with broken input.

\`parse\` and \`serialize\` come from \`@zriz/core\`. add it as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md).

\`Root\` reads \`defaultValue\` once, when it mounts. the component renders nothing until it has read storage for this \`id\`. that empty render unmounts the old cropper, so each photo starts fresh.

\`\`\`tsx
const key = \`zriz-crop:\${id}\`;

const [saved, setSaved] = useState<Saved>();

useEffect(() => {
  setSaved({ id, value: parse(localStorage.getItem(key)) });
}, [id, key]);

if (saved?.id !== id) return null;
\`\`\`

## start from it and save each commit

\`serialize\` writes a json string with a version field and stores the crop as fractions of the photo, so it fits any viewport size. to keep it in a database instead, send the same string with \`fetch\`.

\`Reset\` goes back to \`defaultValue\`, which is now the saved crop. to keep \`Reset\` on the whole photo, pass \`saved.value ?? undefined\` as \`value\` instead. it stays the same object, so it moves the crop only once, see [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md).

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  defaultValue={saved.value ?? undefined}
  onValueCommit={(value) => {
    localStorage.setItem(key, serialize(value));
  }}
>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 640, height: 480 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## the whole file

\`SavedCrop\` takes a photo url and an id to store its crop under:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { parse, serialize } from "@zriz/core";
import type { CropValue } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

type Props = { id: string; src: string };
type Saved = { id: string; value: CropValue | null };

export const SavedCrop = ({ id, src }: Props) => {
  const key = \`zriz-crop:\${id}\`;
  const [saved, setSaved] = useState<Saved>();

  useEffect(() => {
    setSaved({ id, value: parse(localStorage.getItem(key)) });
  }, [id, key]);

  if (saved?.id !== id) return null;

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      src={src}
      defaultValue={saved.value ?? undefined}
      onValueCommit={(value) => {
        localStorage.setItem(key, serialize(value));
      }}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 640, height: 480 }}>
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
      </Cropper.Viewport>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`
"
`;

exports[`content > writes server-crop as markdown 1`] = `
"# server crop

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

send the photo and its crop plan to your server, and cut the full-size original there with sharp.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.

## install sharp

a browser export can come out smaller than the crop. [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) has the size limits, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) explains \`blank\`. sharp has no canvas and never retries smaller, so cut on the server when you need the full‑size crop. sharp refuses photos over about 268 megapixels unless you raise \`limitInputPixels\`.

the route runs on your server and needs \`sharp\`. add it and \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md):

\`\`\`sh
pnpm add sharp @zriz/core
\`\`\`

## send the file and the plan

\`getPixelCrop()\` returns the export plan, a small json object that says which source pixels to keep. it returns \`null\` until the photo loads, so upload waits for \`onLoad\`.

a bad request comes back as text, and the alert shows it. a photo the browser cannot open shows the same alert.

each pick bumps a counter that \`Root\` takes as its \`key\`, so every photo starts from the full frame. [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) explains why.

\`\`\`tsx
const unreadable =
  "this photo does not open. pick a jpeg, png, webp or avif.";

const [picks, setPicks] = useState(0);

const upload = async () => {
  const plan = actions.current?.getPixelCrop();
  if (!file || !plan) return;
  const form = new FormData();
  form.append("file", file);
  form.append("plan", JSON.stringify(plan));
  const response = await fetch("/api/crop", {
    method: "POST",
    body: form,
  }).catch(() => null);
  if (!response) {
    setError("the upload did not reach the server.");
    return;
  }
  if (!response.ok) {
    setError(await response.text());
    return;
  }
  if (result) URL.revokeObjectURL(result);
  setError(null);
  setResult(URL.createObjectURL(await response.blob()));
};

<input
  type="file"
  accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/avif"
  onChange={(event) => {
    setReady(false);
    setError(null);
    if (result) URL.revokeObjectURL(result);
    setResult(null);
    setPicks((count) => count + 1);
    setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null);
  }}
/>

<Cropper.Root
  key={picks}
  actionsRef={actions}
  src={file}
  onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
  onError={() => setError(unreadable)}
>

<button type="button" disabled={!ready} onClick={upload}>
  upload
</button>
\`\`\`

## cut the original with sharp

\`rotate()\` with no angle turns the upload upright from its exif tag. \`toSharp\` takes the plan and the size of that upright image and returns the steps for sharp.

a crop with no straighten and no perspective comes back as \`"extract"\`: a rectangle, a flop and a turn in steps of 90°. any other crop comes back as \`"affine"\`: pad at least 2 px on each side by copying the edge, turn with a bicubic affine, then extract the crop.

in one pipeline sharp runs \`extend()\` after \`affine()\`, and \`affine()\` after \`extract()\` and \`rotate()\`, so the route ends each stage as a raw buffer and opens a new pipeline for the next one. each new pipeline passes \`limitInputPixels: false\`, since the upload already passed the check and a turned crop can grow past it.

\`toSharp\` does not apply perspective. if you set \`perspective\` with \`setValue()\`, export in the browser instead.

\`\`\`ts
type Raw = { data: Buffer; info: OutputInfo };
const open = (image: Raw) =>
  sharp(image.data, {
    raw: image.info,
    limitInputPixels: false,
  });
const raw = (pipeline: Sharp) =>
  pipeline.raw().toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });

const cut = async (input: Buffer, plan: ExportPlan) => {
  const upright = await raw(sharp(input).rotate());
  const steps = toSharp(plan, upright.info);
  if (steps.kind === "extract") {
    return open(upright)
      .extract(steps.extract)
      .flop(steps.flop)
      .rotate(steps.rotate);
  }
  const [[a, b], [c, d]] = steps.affine;
  const padded = await raw(open(upright).extend(steps.extend));
  const turned = await raw(
    open(padded).affine([a, b, c, d], steps.options)
  );
  return open(turned).extract(steps.extract);
};
\`\`\`

## answer the request

the route is a next.js route handler saved as \`app/api/crop/route.ts\`. it answers 400 when the form lacks the file or the plan, 400 when sharp cannot open the file, a photo over the pixel limit included, 422 when the plan does not fit it, and otherwise a jpeg at quality 92 with clear pixels filled white. \`toSharp\` throws a \`RangeError\` on a plan that is not a plain turn and scale of the photo, and the route answers that with 422 too.

the plan comes from the browser, so treat it as client input. a plan that passes \`toSharp\` can still need far more memory than the photo, so keep the route behind your own auth and a rate limit.

\`\`\`ts
export const POST = async (request: Request) => {
  const form = await request.formData();
  const file = form.get("file");
  const plan = form.get("plan");
  if (!(file instanceof File) || typeof plan !== "string") {
    return new Response("send a file and a plan.", {
      status: 400,
    });
  }
  const input = Buffer.from(await file.arrayBuffer());
  const meta = await sharp(input)
    .metadata()
    .catch(() => null);
  if (!meta) {
    return new Response("sharp cannot open this file.", {
      status: 400,
    });
  }
  try {
    const crop = await cut(
      input,
      JSON.parse(plan) as ExportPlan
    );
    const jpeg = await crop
      .flatten({ background: "#ffffff" })
      .jpeg({ quality: 92 })
      .toBuffer();
    return new Response(new Uint8Array(jpeg), {
      headers: { "content-type": "image/jpeg" },
    });
  } catch {
    return new Response("this plan does not fit this photo.", {
      status: 422,
    });
  }
};
\`\`\`

## both files

\`crop-upload.tsx\` runs in the browser:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

const unreadable =
  "this photo does not open. pick a jpeg, png, webp or avif.";

export const CropUpload = () => {
  const [file, setFile] = useState<File | null>(null);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [result, setResult] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [picks, setPicks] = useState(0);
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);

  const upload = async () => {
    const plan = actions.current?.getPixelCrop();
    if (!file || !plan) return;
    const form = new FormData();
    form.append("file", file);
    form.append("plan", JSON.stringify(plan));
    const response = await fetch("/api/crop", {
      method: "POST",
      body: form,
    }).catch(() => null);
    if (!response) {
      setError("the upload did not reach the server.");
      return;
    }
    if (!response.ok) {
      setError(await response.text());
      return;
    }
    if (result) URL.revokeObjectURL(result);
    setError(null);
    setResult(URL.createObjectURL(await response.blob()));
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input
        type="file"
        accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/avif"
        onChange={(event) => {
          setReady(false);
          setError(null);
          if (result) URL.revokeObjectURL(result);
          setResult(null);
          setPicks((count) => count + 1);
          setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null);
        }}
      />
      <Cropper.Root
        key={picks}
        actionsRef={actions}
        src={file}
        onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
        onError={() => setError(unreadable)}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 640, height: 480 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
        <Cropper.Dial
          aria-label="Straighten"
          style={{ width: 361 }}
        >
          <Cropper.DialTicks />
        </Cropper.Dial>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button type="button" disabled={!ready} onClick={upload}>
        upload
      </button>
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
      {result && (
        <img src={result} alt="Crop from the server" />
      )}
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`app/api/crop/route.ts\` runs on the server:

\`\`\`ts
import { toSharp } from "@zriz/core";
import type { ExportPlan } from "@zriz/core";
import sharp from "sharp";
import type { OutputInfo, Sharp } from "sharp";

type Raw = { data: Buffer; info: OutputInfo };
const open = (image: Raw) =>
  sharp(image.data, {
    raw: image.info,
    limitInputPixels: false,
  });
const raw = (pipeline: Sharp) =>
  pipeline.raw().toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });

const cut = async (input: Buffer, plan: ExportPlan) => {
  const upright = await raw(sharp(input).rotate());
  const steps = toSharp(plan, upright.info);
  if (steps.kind === "extract") {
    return open(upright)
      .extract(steps.extract)
      .flop(steps.flop)
      .rotate(steps.rotate);
  }
  const [[a, b], [c, d]] = steps.affine;
  const padded = await raw(open(upright).extend(steps.extend));
  const turned = await raw(
    open(padded).affine([a, b, c, d], steps.options)
  );
  return open(turned).extract(steps.extract);
};

export const POST = async (request: Request) => {
  const form = await request.formData();
  const file = form.get("file");
  const plan = form.get("plan");
  if (!(file instanceof File) || typeof plan !== "string") {
    return new Response("send a file and a plan.", {
      status: 400,
    });
  }
  const input = Buffer.from(await file.arrayBuffer());
  const meta = await sharp(input)
    .metadata()
    .catch(() => null);
  if (!meta) {
    return new Response("sharp cannot open this file.", {
      status: 400,
    });
  }
  try {
    const crop = await cut(
      input,
      JSON.parse(plan) as ExportPlan
    );
    const jpeg = await crop
      .flatten({ background: "#ffffff" })
      .jpeg({ quality: 92 })
      .toBuffer();
    return new Response(new Uint8Array(jpeg), {
      headers: { "content-type": "image/jpeg" },
    });
  } catch {
    return new Response("this plan does not fit this photo.", {
      status: 422,
    });
  }
};
\`\`\`
"
`;

exports[`content > writes styling as markdown 1`] = `
"# styling

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

the parts carry no colour. paint them with the optional stylesheet, your own css or tailwind.

## add the stylesheet

import the stylesheet once. [bundle size](https://zriz.dev/docs/bundle-size.md) lists its weight:

\`\`\`ts
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
\`\`\`

| part | what the stylesheet sets |
| - | - |
| \`Viewport\` | \`overflow: clip\` and a grab cursor |
| \`Mask\` | the scrim colour. it fades to 60% during a gesture |
| \`Area\` | a 1 px outline inside the crop |
| \`Handle\` | a corner mark of two short lines or an edge bar, and a resize cursor. corners hide with \`shape="circle"\` |
| \`Grid\` | white lines that fade in during a gesture |
| \`Dial\` | 40 px height, sideways clip, a grab cursor, centre line, tick colours, the zero dot |

it paints nothing on \`Zoom\`, the buttons or the aspect items. the demos on this site style those themselves. [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md) shows how to size the box.

every rule sits in \`:where()\` inside \`@layer components.zriz\`, so any class of yours wins in any import order, tailwind included. \`@zriz/core/styles.css\` is the same file for [vanilla js](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md).

## style a part by its state

\`className\` takes a string, or a function that gets the part's state:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Grid
  className={(state) =>
    state.grid === "fine" ? "opacity-50" : undefined
  }
/>
\`\`\`

return \`undefined\` from the function for no class. to swap the element itself, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md).

## style with data attributes

every part's outer element carries \`data-zriz-item\` with its part name in kebab case, such as \`aspect-item\` or \`dial-ticks\`. the ticks and grid lines inside carry none, so select them through their part, as [dial](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md) shows. \`Root\` also marks the gesture in progress with \`data-rotating\`, \`data-panning\`, \`data-pinching\`, \`data-resizing\` or \`data-zooming\`. this rule hides the handles while the dial is dragged or thrown:

\`\`\`css
[data-rotating] [data-zriz-item="handle"] {
  opacity: 0;
}
\`\`\`

each component page lists the attributes that part sets.

## change the colours

set the colour variables on \`Root\`, on one part or on any ancestor. this one makes the scrim white in light mode and darker in dark mode, and the accent blue:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="root"] {
  --zriz-scrim: light-dark(#ffffffd9, #000000d9);
  --zriz-accent: #0a84ff;
}
\`\`\`

| variable | light, dark | what it sets |
| - | - | - |
| \`--zriz-scrim\` | \`#f7f7f7eb\`, \`#000000c7\` | the mask outside the crop |
| \`--zriz-line\` | \`#000\`, \`#fff\` | outline, handle marks, dial centre line, major ticks, zero dot |
| \`--zriz-grid\` | \`#fff9\` in both | grid lines |
| \`--zriz-tick\` | \`#9b9b9b\`, \`#666\` | minor ticks on the dial |
| \`--zriz-accent\` | \`#fdca00\` in both | dial centre line while you drag the dial |

write a value with \`light-dark()\` to keep both schemes, or a single colour to use it in both.

## follow dark mode

the parts follow the page's \`color-scheme\`. the light values above apply in light mode and the dark values in dark mode:

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/styling.

with this rule on the page, the parts follow the system setting:

\`\`\`css
:root {
  color-scheme: light dark;
}
\`\`\`

to pin one part, put \`data-theme="dark"\` or the class \`dark\` on the part itself. it wins over a themed wrapper. \`light\` works on the part itself, but its children stay dark inside a dark wrapper:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Dial data-theme="dark" aria-label="Straighten" />
\`\`\`

in forced colours mode, the stylesheet switches lines, grid and ticks to \`CanvasText\` and the accent to \`Highlight\`, so the crop stays visible.

## style with tailwind

without the stylesheet, utilities paint everything, the viewport's clip included. tailwind's \`data-*\` variants read the state attributes:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={file}>
  <Cropper.Viewport className="h-80 overflow-clip">
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask className="bg-black/60" />
    <Cropper.Area
      aria-label="Crop area"
      className="ring-1 ring-white data-interacting:ring-2"
    />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

the mask turns 60% black, and the crop gets a white ring that thickens during a gesture.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes undo as markdown 1`] = `
"# undo

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

an undo button for the crop, built from what each commit hands you.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/undo.

## remember the crop before each gesture

\`details.previous\` holds the crop from before the gesture. one gesture is one commit, however many frames it took. the stack keeps the last 50.

\`remember\` skips commits with two reasons. \`history\` is the undo itself, and \`coverage\` is zriz fitting the crop to a new photo or new limits.

\`\`\`tsx
const skipped = new Set(["history", "coverage"]);

const [history, setHistory] = useState<CropValue[]>([]);

const remember = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) => {
  if (skipped.has(details.reason)) return;
  setHistory((stack) =>
    [...stack, details.previous].slice(-50)
  );
};
\`\`\`

## step back

\`setValue()\` commits like any other change, with the reason you give it. the button stays off while the stack is empty.

for redo, keep a second stack. \`undo\` pushes \`actions.current.getValue()\` onto it before it steps back. a \`redo\` function pops it, pushes the current crop onto the undo stack and calls \`setValue()\` with \`history\` too. \`remember\` clears the redo stack after the reason check, so a new gesture drops it.

\`\`\`tsx
const undo = () => {
  const previous = history.at(-1);
  if (!previous) return;
  setHistory(history.slice(0, -1));
  actions.current?.setValue(previous, { reason: "history" });
};

<button
  type="button"
  disabled={history.length === 0}
  onClick={undo}
>
  undo
</button>
\`\`\`

## wire it to the cropper

\`onValueCommit\` hands every commit to \`remember\`. crops from the last photo make no sense on the next one, so \`onLoad\` clears the stack.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  actionsRef={actions}
  src={src}
  onLoad={() => setHistory([])}
  onValueCommit={remember}
>
\`\`\`

## the whole file

\`UndoEditor\` adds the button under the dial:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type {
  CommitDetails,
  Cropper as CropperApi,
  CropValue,
} from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

const skipped = new Set(["history", "coverage"]);

export const UndoEditor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const [history, setHistory] = useState<CropValue[]>([]);

  const remember = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) => {
    if (skipped.has(details.reason)) return;
    setHistory((stack) =>
      [...stack, details.previous].slice(-50)
    );
  };

  const undo = () => {
    const previous = history.at(-1);
    if (!previous) return;
    setHistory(history.slice(0, -1));
    actions.current?.setValue(previous, { reason: "history" });
  };

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      actionsRef={actions}
      src={src}
      onLoad={() => setHistory([])}
      onValueCommit={remember}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 640, height: 480 }}>
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ width: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={history.length === 0}
        onClick={undo}
      >
        undo
      </button>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`
"
`;

exports[`content > writes value as markdown 1`] = `
"# value

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

a plain object that describes the crop. read it to save a crop, and set it to restore or control one.

a square crop of a 4032 × 3024 photo, half its height, right of the middle and a little above it, straightened 3°:

\`\`\`ts
import type { CropValue } from "@zriz/react";

const value: CropValue = {
  center: { x: 0.6, y: 0.45 },
  size: { width: 0.375, height: 0.5 },
  rotation: 3,
  quarterTurns: 0,
  mirrored: false,
  perspective: { vertical: 0, horizontal: 0 },
  aspect: 1,
};
\`\`\`

outside react, import the type from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md).

\`onValueCommit\`, \`getValue()\` and \`useCropperValue\` all hand you this object. \`Root\` and \`createCropper\` take the whole object as \`value\`, or part of it as \`defaultValue\`.

## fields

- \`center\` \`Point\` = \`{ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 }\`

  marks the middle of the crop. 0.5, 0.5 is the middle of the photo
- \`size\` \`Size\` = \`{ width: 1, height: 1 }\`

  sets the crop's width and height as fractions of the photo's sides
- \`rotation\` \`number\` = \`0\`

  straightens the photo, in degrees from −45 to 45. a big \`minSize\` on a small photo lowers the limit, see \`getRotationLimit()\` on [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md)
- \`quarterTurns\` \`0 | 1 | 2 | 3\` = \`0\`

  counts clockwise 90° turns. one counter-clockwise turn reads 3
- \`mirrored\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  swaps left and right. each flip toggles it and negates \`rotation\`. a left to right flip also turns a \`quarterTurns\` of 1 into 3, and a top to bottom flip turns 0 into 2
- \`perspective\` \`object\` = \`{ vertical: 0, horizontal: 0 }\`

  tilts the photo by \`vertical\` and \`horizontal\` degrees. no part or gesture sets it, only \`setValue()\`, \`value\` and \`defaultValue\`. keep each within −30 to 30. \`parse\` clamps to that range, \`setValue()\` does not
- \`aspect\` \`Aspect\` = \`"free"\`

  locks the crop's shape to a ratio such as \`16 / 9\`, to \`"original"\` for the photo's own shape, or to nothing with \`"free"\`

\`center\` and \`size\` are fractions of the photo after its quarter turns, never pixels. the same value fits a 400 px preview and the 4032 px original.

zriz measures each side on its own. the largest square crop of a 4032 × 3024 photo reads \`size: { width: 0.75, height: 1 }\`.

there is no zoom field. zoom follows from \`size\`, and \`getZoom()\` returns it: 1 is the largest crop that fits.

## see how zriz fixes a bad value

zriz keeps every value you set valid. it clamps the angle, reshapes a \`size\` that misses its \`aspect\` at the same area, grows a crop below \`minSize\` or past \`maxZoom\`, and shrinks or moves a crop that would show an empty corner. ask for a 90° straighten and you get the limit:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
});
cropper.setValue({ rotation: 90 });
cropper.getValue().rotation; // 45
\`\`\`

a field that is not a usable number keeps the value it had, and a write with a usable field that still cannot make a crop, such as \`setRotation(NaN)\`, is ignored. an \`aspect\` that is not a number from 0.000001 to 1000000 counts as \`"free"\`. an option that is not a positive number falls back to its default, but any \`maxZoom\` below 1, zero and negatives included, counts as 1.

a value from zriz is frozen, so one reader cannot change it for the next. copy it to change a field: \`setValue({ ...value, rotation: 2 })\`.

before a photo loads, zriz stores a value as you set it and fixes it when the photo arrives.

## control the angle from react state

pass \`rotation\` and update it in \`onValueChange\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useState } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const [rotation, setRotation] = useState(0);
  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      src={src}
      rotation={rotation}
      onValueChange={(value) => setRotation(value.rotation)}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ width: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`rotation\`, \`zoom\` and \`aspect\` each control one part of the crop. a new prop moves the crop and calls \`onValueChange\` with what zriz applied, so your state matches the screen. a rotation of 60 reports 45. zoom runs from 1 up to \`maxZoom\`. the move also commits, so \`onValueCommit\` runs too. a prop applied as the photo arrives is silent.

\`value\` sets the whole object without calling either callback. each new object moves the crop, so keep it stable or update it from \`onValueChange\`. \`Reset\` still goes to \`defaultValue\`, not to the last \`value\`.

during a gesture and until it commits, zriz ignores a new \`value\`, \`rotation\` or \`zoom\`, so a slow render cannot fight the finger. to snap the crop after a gesture, write it in \`onValueCommit\`. a new \`aspect\` applies at once. the \`aspect\` and \`rotation\` from the first render become the reset target.

## turn it into text and back

save a crop as text and read it back:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper, parse, serialize } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper();
const text = serialize(cropper.getValue());
const saved = parse(text);
\`\`\`

\`serialize\` adds a version field, \`v: 1\`. \`parse\` takes the text or an object you already parsed, and returns \`null\` when it is not json or not an object. it fills missing fields with defaults, clamps \`rotation\` to 45 and \`perspective\` to 30 each way, and turns a bad \`aspect\` into \`"free"\`. the full flow is on [saving state](https://zriz.dev/docs/saving-state.md).

## helpers

a constant and pure functions from \`@zriz/core\`:

| export | what it does |
| - | - |
| \`defaultValue\` | is the whole photo, level, with a free aspect |
| \`valuesEqual\` | takes \`(a, b)\` and returns \`true\` when every field matches |
| \`changedFields\` | takes \`(a, b)\` and returns the names of the fields that differ |
| \`aspectRatio\` | takes \`(aspect, image, quarterTurns)\` and returns the ratio as a number, or \`null\` for a free aspect |
| \`valueToState\` | takes \`(value, image, focal?)\` and returns the crop in image pixels. \`focal\` is a length in pixels and defaults to a 26 mm lens on the photo's long side |
| \`stateToValue\` | takes \`(state, aspect)\` and turns a crop in image pixels back into a value |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes vanilla-js as markdown 1`] = `
"# vanilla js

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

run the same cropper in plain javascript or any framework, with \`createCropper\` and the dom binders.

## install the core package

add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). it has no dependencies, not even react.

## write the markup

give each element its \`data-zriz-item\`, so the stylesheet finds it, and give the viewport a width and a height. the binders set no role or name on the crop area, so write them in the markup:

\`\`\`html
<div id="cropper" data-zriz-item="root">
  <div
    id="viewport"
    data-zriz-item="viewport"
    style="width: 480px; height: 360px; padding: 24px"
  >
    <div id="photo" data-zriz-item="image"></div>
    <div id="mask" data-zriz-item="mask"></div>
    <div
      id="area"
      data-zriz-item="area"
      role="group"
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Crop area"
    ></div>
  </div>
  <div
    id="dial"
    data-zriz-item="dial"
    aria-label="Straighten"
    style="position: relative"
  >
    <div id="ticks" data-zriz-item="dial-ticks"></div>
  </div>
</div>
<input id="file" type="file" accept="image/*" />
<p id="status" role="alert"></p>
\`\`\`

## bind the parts

create a cropper, bind each element, add the four corner handles, and attach the photo when the user picks a file:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";
import {
  attachImage,
  bindArea,
  bindHandle,
  bindMask,
  bindPhoto,
  bindState,
  bindViewport,
  loadImage,
} from "@zriz/core/dom";
import "@zriz/core/styles.css";

const byId = (id: string) =>
  document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;

export const cropper = createCropper({
  defaultValue: { aspect: 4 / 3 },
});

export const cleanups = [
  bindState(cropper, byId("cropper")),
  bindViewport(cropper, byId("viewport")),
  bindPhoto(cropper, byId("photo")),
  bindMask(cropper, byId("mask")),
  bindArea(cropper, byId("area")),
];

const corners = [
  "top-left",
  "top-right",
  "bottom-left",
  "bottom-right",
] as const;
for (const corner of corners) {
  const handle = document.createElement("div");
  handle.dataset.zrizItem = "handle";
  handle.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
  byId("area").append(handle);
  cleanups.push(bindHandle(cropper, handle, corner));
}

byId("file").addEventListener("change", async (event) => {
  const input = event.target as HTMLInputElement;
  const file = input.files?.[0];
  if (!file) return;
  try {
    attachImage(cropper, await loadImage(file));
  } catch {
    byId("status").textContent = "this photo does not open.";
  }
});
\`\`\`

each binder writes geometry inline and returns its cleanup. \`bindHandle\` also takes \`"top"\`, \`"right"\`, \`"bottom"\` and \`"left"\` for the edge bars. after you pick a file, the photo shows under a 4:3 crop. the handles have no layout until the next section places them.

when a file does not open, \`loadImage\` rejects with a \`ZrizError\`, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists its codes.

## place the handles

\`bindHandle\` wires the drag but leaves layout to you. this css centres a 44 px hit box on each corner and on the middle of each edge, and \`--zriz-handle-size\` on the area resizes it as on [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="handle"] {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: var(--zriz-handle-size, 44px);
  height: var(--zriz-handle-size, 44px);
  translate: -50% -50%;
}
[data-edge$="right"] {
  left: 100%;
}
[data-edge^="bottom"] {
  top: 100%;
}
[data-edge="top"],
[data-edge="bottom"] {
  left: 50%;
}
[data-edge="left"],
[data-edge="right"] {
  top: 50%;
}
\`\`\`

## move the crop with keys

\`bindArea\` moves the crop but listens to no keys. pass the area's events to \`areaKeyHandlers\`:

\`\`\`ts
import { areaKeyHandlers } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cropper } from "./cropper";

const area = document.getElementById("area") as HTMLElement;
const keys = areaKeyHandlers(cropper);

area.onkeydown = keys.onKeyDown;
area.onkeyup = keys.onKeyUp;
area.onblur = keys.onBlur;

export const unbind = () => {
  keys.release();
};
\`\`\`

the keys are the ones on [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md). call \`keys.release()\` when you unbind, so a key held at that moment does not lock the cropper. \`zoomHandlers\` and \`handleHandlers\` have the same \`release\`.

## add the dial

\`bindDial\` handles drags, keys and the slider's aria values. \`bindDialTicks\` fills the strip with 41 ticks and moves it:

\`\`\`ts
import { bindDial, bindDialTicks } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cleanups, cropper } from "./cropper";

const dial = document.getElementById("dial") as HTMLElement;
const ticks = document.getElementById("ticks") as HTMLElement;

cleanups.push(
  bindDial(cropper, dial),
  bindDialTicks(cropper, ticks)
);
\`\`\`

pass \`{ dir: "rtl" }\` as the third argument of \`bindDial\` to swap the arrows and the drag.

## add a zoom slider

zoom has no single binder. give the element \`role="slider"\`, \`tabindex="0"\`, an \`aria-label\`, a size, \`position: relative\` and \`touch-action: none\`, and draw the thumb yourself. the stylesheet paints nothing on zoom:

\`\`\`html
<div
  id="zoom"
  data-zriz-item="zoom"
  role="slider"
  tabindex="0"
  aria-label="Zoom"
  style="
    position: relative;
    width: 200px;
    height: 24px;
    touch-action: none;
  "
>
  <div
    id="thumb"
    data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"
    style="
      top: 4px;
      width: 16px;
      height: 16px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      background: currentColor;
      translate: -50%;
    "
  ></div>
</div>
\`\`\`

then hand its events to \`zoomHandlers\`:

\`\`\`ts
import {
  bindZoomThumb,
  zoomAria,
  zoomHandlers,
} from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cleanups, cropper } from "./cropper";

const zoom = document.getElementById("zoom") as HTMLElement;
const thumb = document.getElementById("thumb") as HTMLElement;
const handlers = zoomHandlers(cropper);

zoom.onpointerdown = handlers.onPointerDown;
zoom.onpointermove = handlers.onPointerMove;
zoom.onpointerup = handlers.onPointerUp;
zoom.onpointercancel = handlers.onPointerCancel;
zoom.onlostpointercapture = handlers.onLostPointerCapture;
zoom.onkeydown = handlers.onKeyDown;
zoom.onkeyup = handlers.onKeyUp;
zoom.onblur = handlers.onBlur;

cleanups.push(
  zoomAria(cropper, zoom),
  bindZoomThumb(cropper, thumb)
);
\`\`\`

\`zoomAria\` keeps the slider's values current, and \`bindZoomThumb\` moves the thumb from 0% at 1× to 100% at the largest zoom.

## read the crop

the \`commit\` event fires once per gesture with the crop value and the export plan, which is \`null\` until a photo loads:

\`\`\`ts
import { cropper } from "./cropper";

cropper.on("commit", (value, details) => {
  console.log(value.size, details.pixelCrop);
});
\`\`\`

to turn the crop into a file, pass the cropper to \`exportCrop\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). a photo nobody touches sends no \`commit\`, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md).

## connect the rest

| part | how to wire it |
| - | - |
| dial value | \`bindDialValue(cropper, element, format)\` writes the angle as text. without \`format\`, it writes whole degrees |
| grid | no binder. \`bindState\` writes \`data-grid\` on the root as \`"none"\`, \`"coarse"\` or \`"fine"\`. draw the lines in css |
| buttons | no binder. call \`rotate90()\`, \`flip()\`, \`reset()\` or \`setAspect(1)\` on the cropper from a click |
| your own img | \`bindImage(cropper, element)\` sizes and moves an element of yours with the photo. you set its \`src\` |
| round crop | pass \`"circle"\` as the third argument of \`bindMask\` and \`{ shape: "circle" }\` as the third argument of \`bindArea\`. set \`aspect: 1\` in \`defaultValue\`. the stylesheet hides the corner handles when the area has \`data-shape="circle"\`, which \`bindArea\` leaves to your markup |
| wheel and twist | pass \`{ wheel: "zoom", rotateGesture: false }\` as the third argument of \`bindViewport\`. \`wheel\` also takes \`"pan"\` and \`"none"\` |
| motion | call \`viewOf(cropper).setOptions({ motion: "reduce", recentreDelay: 800 })\`. \`motion\` also takes \`"auto"\` and \`"always"\`. each call replaces both options |
| live variables | pass \`{ liveVariables: true }\` as the third argument of \`bindArea\`, see [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md) |
| announcements | no binder. on \`commit\`, write your own text into an element with \`role="status"\`, and point the area's \`aria-describedby\` at your instructions |

## tear it down

call every cleanup, detach the photo so its memory is freed, then destroy the cropper:

\`\`\`ts
import { attachImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cleanups, cropper } from "./cropper";

for (const cleanup of cleanups) cleanup();
attachImage(cropper, null);
cropper.destroy();
\`\`\`

\`destroy()\` also runs each plugin's \`destroy\`.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes viewport as markdown 1`] = `
"# viewport

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Viewport\` takes the gestures, \`Image\` draws the photo in it and \`Mask\` dims everything outside the crop.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.

## anatomy

\`Viewport\` goes inside \`Root\`, with \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Area\` inside it. its width fills the parent like any block, so give it a height:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
  <Cropper.Image />
  <Cropper.Mask />
  <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
</Cropper.Viewport>
\`\`\`

zriz makes it \`position: relative\` when it is static and sets \`touch-action: none\`, so the page does not scroll under a finger. \`Image\` clips the photo to the box by itself. the stylesheet also clips the viewport, so a handle at its edge never hangs outside it.

## leave room around the crop

zriz fits the crop inside the viewport's padding. without padding the crop touches the edges, and the clip cuts the handles in half. 24 px leaves room for the 44 px hit box to grab, see [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="viewport"] {
  padding: 24px;
}
\`\`\`

the crop fits again whenever the viewport changes size.

## put it in a dialog

centre the dialog with \`inset: 0\` and \`margin: auto\`. a percent translate on an odd size puts everything inside on a half pixel, and at 1x chromium blurs the crop's outline.

## choose what the wheel does

a plain wheel or two-finger scroll pans the photo. \`wheel="zoom"\` makes it zoom, and \`wheel="none"\` leaves it to the page. pinch and ctrl + wheel zoom in every mode:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Viewport wheel="none" style={{ height: 400 }} />
\`\`\`

## turn off the two-finger twist

a twist straightens the photo once it passes 5°. to keep two fingers for pinch only:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Viewport
  rotateGesture={false}
  style={{ height: 400 }}
/>
\`\`\`

## fade the photo in when it loads

\`Image\` gets \`data-ready\` once the cropper knows the photo's size: when it decodes, or at once when you pass \`image\` to \`Root\`. hide it until then:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="image"] {
  transition: opacity 0.2s;
}
[data-zriz-item="image"]:not([data-ready]) {
  opacity: 0;
}
\`\`\`

## darken the mask

the stylesheet paints the mask with \`--zriz-scrim\`. set it to any colour:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="mask"] {
  --zriz-scrim: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7);
}
\`\`\`

## what each gesture does

\`Viewport\` handles pointer, touch and wheel input with no extra code:

| input | what it does |
| - | - |
| drag | pans the photo under the crop |
| pinch, trackpad pinch | zooms, with a rubber band at both ends |
| twist two fingers | straightens once the twist passes 5° |
| wheel, trackpad scroll | pans by default. at the edge the page scrolls on. \`wheel\` changes it |
| ctrl + wheel | zooms, whatever \`wheel\` is set to |
| escape during a drag | cancels the drag and puts the photo back |

a press on a button, link, input or other control inside \`Viewport\` starts no pan, so its click still lands. the same goes for content you portal out of it.

a twist counts as a pinch, so \`Root\` gets \`data-pinching\` and the thirds grid shows. a trackpad twist works only in safari. the crop's keys are on [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md).

## api

### \`Viewport\`

renders a \`<div>\` inside \`Root\`. gesture attributes like \`data-panning\` go on \`Root\`. every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ interacting }\`.

- \`wheel\` \`"pan" | "zoom" | "none"\` = \`"pan"\`

  what a plain wheel or two-finger scroll does
- \`rotateGesture\` \`boolean\` = \`true\`

  lets a two-finger twist straighten the photo

### \`Image\`

renders a \`<div>\` inside \`Viewport\`. it holds an \`<img>\` that moves while you drag and a \`<canvas>\` drawn sharp at rest. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ ready }\`.

- \`alt\` \`string\` = \`""\`

  names the photo for screen readers. leave it empty when the crop is described elsewhere

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-ready\` | once the cropper knows the photo's size: after it decodes, or at once with \`image\` on \`Root\` |
| \`data-sharp\` | while the sharp canvas frame shows |

### \`Mask\`

renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` inside \`Viewport\`. it has no props of its own. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ shape }\`.

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-shape\` | \`"rect"\` or \`"circle"\`, from \`Root\` |
| \`data-interacting\` | during a gesture and for a short hold after it, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) |

| variable | what it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-scrim\` | paints the mask outside the crop. [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |

the stylesheet fades it during a gesture, see [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md).
"
`;

exports[`content > writes zoom as markdown 1`] = `
"# zoom

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

a zoom slider and step buttons, for people with no wheel or pinch.

the demo runs on the page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/zoom.

## anatomy

put \`ZoomThumb\` inside \`Zoom\`. the steps go anywhere inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.ZoomStep factor={0.8} aria-label="Zoom out">
  −
</Cropper.ZoomStep>
<Cropper.Zoom
  aria-label="Zoom"
  style={{ width: 240, height: 24 }}
>
  <Cropper.ZoomThumb />
</Cropper.Zoom>
<Cropper.ZoomStep factor={1.25} aria-label="Zoom in">
  +
</Cropper.ZoomStep>
\`\`\`

zriz paints nothing here and gives these parts no size. give \`Zoom\` a width and a height, then style the track and the thumb. a press on the track jumps the zoom to that point. [full editor](https://zriz.dev/docs/full-editor.md) paints a track and a thumb you can copy.

## centre the thumb

zriz sets the thumb's \`inset-inline-start\` on a log scale, so 2× sits 26% along a 15× track. this centres a round thumb on that point:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"] {
  top: 4px;
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  translate: -50% 0;
  background: #111;
}
\`\`\`

in a right-to-left layout, use \`translate: 50% 0\`.

## stop the slider at 5×

pass the same \`max\` to \`Zoom\` and \`ZoomThumb\`. pinch still reaches 15×:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Zoom
  max={5}
  aria-label="Zoom"
  style={{ width: 240, height: 24 }}
>
  <Cropper.ZoomThumb max={5} />
</Cropper.Zoom>
\`\`\`

to cap every way of zooming, set \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\` instead. both parts follow it.

## disable a step at the limit

at 1× and at \`maxZoom\`, a press does nothing, but the button stays enabled. \`useZoom\` tells you where the zoom is. render this inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useZoom } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";

export const ZoomSteps = () => {
  const zoom = useZoom();
  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.ZoomStep
        factor={0.8}
        aria-label="Zoom out"
        disabled={zoom.value <= zoom.min}
      >
        −
      </Cropper.ZoomStep>
      <Cropper.ZoomStep
        factor={1.25}
        aria-label="Zoom in"
        disabled={zoom.value >= zoom.max}
      >
        +
      </Cropper.ZoomStep>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## build your own slider

\`useZoom\` hands a slider of your own the zoom and a commit callback. this one is a range input. render it inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useZoom } from "@zriz/react";

export const ZoomSlider = () => {
  const zoom = useZoom();
  return (
    <input
      type="range"
      aria-label="Zoom"
      min={zoom.min}
      max={zoom.max}
      step={0.01}
      value={zoom.value}
      onChange={(e) =>
        zoom.onValueChange(e.target.valueAsNumber)
      }
      onPointerUp={zoom.onValueCommitted}
      onPointerCancel={zoom.onValueCommitted}
      onKeyUp={zoom.onValueCommitted}
    />
  );
};
\`\`\`

a range input moves in even steps, so 2× sits 7% along a 15× track. \`Zoom\` uses a log scale.

[hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md) lists every field \`useZoom\` returns.

## keyboard

these keys work while \`Zoom\` has focus:

| key | what it does |
| - | - |
| \`left\` or \`right\` | move 1/20 of the track. right zooms in, left in rtl |
| \`up\` or \`down\` | move 1/20 of the track. up zooms in |
| \`shift\` + \`arrow keys\` | move half the track |
| \`page up\` or \`page down\` | move half the track |
| \`home\` or \`end\` | go to 1× or to \`max\` |

each step multiplies the zoom by the same amount, about 1.14× on a 15× track.

## api

### \`Zoom\`

renders a \`<div role="slider">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ dragging }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  names the slider for screen readers. development builds warn without it
- \`max\` \`number\`

  the zoom at the end of the track. defaults to \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\`
- \`format\` \`Intl.NumberFormatOptions\`

  how screen readers hear the zoom. by default 2 reads as \`"200%"\`

| attribute | when |
| - | - |
| \`data-dragging\` | while a pointer holds the track or a \`useZoom\` slider moves |

### \`ZoomThumb\`

renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` inside \`Zoom\`. zriz writes \`position: absolute\` and \`inset-inline-start\`, from 0% at 1× to 100% at \`max\`.

- \`max\` \`number\`

  the zoom at the end of the track. defaults to \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\`. keep it equal to \`max\` on \`Zoom\`

### \`ZoomStep\`

renders a \`<button type="button">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. each press jumps at once, with no animation and no grid.

- \`factor\` \`number\` required

  multiplies the zoom on each press. below 1 zooms out, so 0.8 undoes 1.25
"
`;
